(Duncan Entwisle) wrote:

>I've followed the instructions on how to set a new password for SYSTEM
>as described in the FAQ, however I'm having problems.

Okay, I managed to get where I wanted using a round about route.
I'll describe my method here in case its useful for anyone else, or
alternatively if I've made a hideous blunder someone might come to my
aid :-)

Having gotten to the dollar prompt...

SPAWN
SET DEFAULT SYS$SYSTEM
COPY SYSUAF.DAT _SYSUAF.DAT
DELETE SYSUAF.DAT

I then restarted the system, booting in normally. It prompted me for a
username, and then password (twice) - it looks like you can put pretty
much anything in here, and it lets you in.

SET DEFAULT SYS$SYSTEM
AUTHORIZE
MODIFY SYSTEM /PASSWORD=whatever
EXIT
LOGOUT

I was then able to login properly as SYSTEM. This doesn't deviate from
the FAQ version too much, but I'd still like to know why the original
mechanism didn't work for me if anyone has any ideas?

Thanks.
Duncan.



