It seems none of the posters here actually saw the demo at IDF that is
referred to in the Intel press release. I attended the IDF keynote address,
where they gave the demo and the 1 GHz demo was lame.

At IDF Intel just said that the demo was of a 0.25u PIII part, and didn't
mention any special cooling at all. Of course any thinking person had to
know it was massively cooled as the actual system could not be seen because
it was backstage.

The press release claims they were running the PowerPoint in the background
and the CPU frequency meter in the foreground. I don't recall that PowerPoint
ran in the background at all. The demo started at 900 MHz and then they
cranked up the frequency to 1002 MHz as indicated by the CPU frequency meter
program. While this was happening, no "real" SW was running. The whole thing
was over in a minute. So what Intel demonstrated was that they can have a
super cooled PIII running 1 GHz while sitting "at the command prompt" for
one minute without crashing. At least in their previous demo, they were
running real SW on an overclocked PII until it crashed at 803 MHz (at least
this is what was reported in the press). So, their demo this time was even
more worthless than in the past. I can't understand how anyone would get
exited over that.

At IDF, Intel didn't talk about any commercial parts running faster than 700
MHz in 1999. When talking about a 1 GHz part next year it sounded more like
the very end of 2000.


-- Norbert

