>:Both OS's are installed but it seems that booting Digital UNIX
>:wipes out critical pieces of the VMS file system.  The standard
>:advice we get is "don't mount the VMS file system from Digital
>:UNIX", but we don't.  Evidently this has something to do with the
>:BIOS "autodiscovering" and "automounting" file systems before DU
>:even starts.
>
>  The SRM is used both by OpenVMS and Tru64 UNIX.  The SRM
>  console does not automount the file system, and it does not
>  have a "mount" construct.  It knows how to transfer control
>  to the operating system, and it knows how to tell the
>  operating system about the system configuration.
>
>:The two file systems are on separate physical disks.  Of course
>:we could always pull one or the other out physically when booting,
>:but a less invasive procedure would be preferred.
>
>  You will want to ask the more UNIX-knowledgable folks, not
>  the OpenVMS folks.  (Hence the comp.sys.dec posting....)
>
>  This is very likely something in how the UNIX system was
>  generated, or how it is configured -- it is not the console
>  and it is not OpenVMS.  Try going as far into the UNIX
>  bootstrap as single-user mode, and look around.
>
> -------------------------- pure personal
opinion ---------------------------
> Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com
>

Seem simple enough to me, I'm doing something similar on a 1000A for some
testing here.

Assume that the VMS system is on disk dkc0
Assume that the unix system is on dkc1
Assume you are at the console prompt.

Set environment variable os_type to VMS (>>> set os_type VMS)
re-initialise the system
Set bootdef_dev dkc0
Set bootflags and bootfile to something useful
boot VMS, no problem.

Set environment variable os_type to UNIX (>>> set os_type UNIX) ,
re-initialise the system
Set bootdef_dev dkc1
Set bootflags and bootfile to something useful
boot unix, no problem.

However I don't know if when you did the installations you had both disks
installed,
but if you did then try installing Unix without the dkc0 disk installed, and
VMS without the dkc1 disk installed, then plug em both back in and use the
information above.








