>Bryan Lawrence wrote:

> Is there a hobbiest license available for RT11 or RSX11?  I remember seeing
> something about a year or so ago but I don't remember where.  Can someone
> show me the way to the end of the tunnel.

Jerome Fine replies:

Yes!  The license is for use under an emulator which has been written by
DEC.  There are two emulators which qualify, as far as I know:

(a)  The Viking emulator which runs on a Alpha and is likely more
      expensive than most hobby users would want to pay.  In general
      the hobby license is targeted toward the second option.

(b)  The Supnik emulator written by Bob Supnik from DEC (at the time)
      which I understand is written in "C" and can be compiled to run under
      a number of different systems, most of which are UNIX based.
      However, I have seen at least one version which has been compiled
      to run under MS-DOS/W95/W98.

The hobby license is for V5.3 of RT-11 and the terms of the license do not
allow you to run under any other emulator or on real PDP-11 hardware.

As far as I know, all of the OSs which are allowed to legally execute
under a DEC emulator on a hobby basis are not Y2K compliant.
For sure, V5.3 of RT-11 is not.

If you go to deja news, there are a number of posts on this subject
over the past year.  Most were on the news groups "alt.sys.pdp11"
and "vmsnet.pdp-11".  If you would like more information, please
ask.  V5.3 of RT-11 is available if you want to use it on a hobby
basis.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
