In comp.os.vms Pete Humble <spamfree@peet.at.dircon.dot.co.dot.uk> wrote:
> I thought that all DECservers came with software licenses automatically.

  Yes, licenses for the version currently shipping when the server was sold
by DEC. DEC has stated in the past that in order to legally install updates
on more than one DECserver, you need to have the first server on a software
support contract (update license and media kit) and additional servers of
the same model on a contract for update licenses. As I recall, this was called
additional node or similar.

  For servers that can run multiple images, this is more complex - servers
that were licenses to run the DS700 software need a license upgrade to legally
run the DNAS software. This was done by either having the servers on support
or buying the upgrade kit which included a new SIMM and a new license.

  There's also the license transfer issue. I'm not sure what Digipaq's current
thinking on this is.

  This is all silly - just about every other terminal server company offers
free lifetime software upgrades.

