  dhinds@zen.stanford.edu (David Hinds) wrote:
> Not to mention, Intel isn't the first to make the 1GHz demonstration:
> IBM showed off a 1GHz stripped powerpc core more than a year ago.

The key word here is "stripped".  The IBM chip had less than a million
transistors (maybe only tens of thousands?  I don't recall).  The Intel
chip had the full 7.5 million of a Pentium II.  Also interesting is that
the IBM chip was made with an advanced manufacturing process that won't
even be used for commercial chips for another year at least.  The Intel
chip was produced with their standard .25 micron process.  Sure, it may
have been hand-picked and frozen, but it was a commercial product.

