"Dennis O'Connor" <dmoc@primenet.com> writes:

> It's worse than that.  Even with plenty of O2 left to breath,
> CO2 can mess you up bad.  IIRC, your body decides
> when to take it's next breath based not on a lack of
> oxygen in the blood, but on the build up of too much
> CO2 in the blood.  So if the ambient CO2 concentration
> gets too high, you'll start breathing very rapidly and
> strenuously, which will cause you to hyperventilate
> and pass out from too much O2 in the blood.

No, the latter can't happen: in healthy humans, blood is saturated with
oxygen. There is competition between oxygen and CO2, but you need very
high levels of CO2 for that; CO is much worse in this regard, and can't be
driven out easily even by breathing 100% oxygen. If you hyperventilate,
you _remove_ too much CO2 from the blood; this ups its pH, and the brain
doesn't like that and you faint. Too much CO2 drives the CO2 into the opposite
direction (i.e., acidic), and this is, AFAIK, why you shouldn't try to breathe
too much of it.

	Jan



