What is this "memory was not on the IO path"??? As far as I am concernced
intel chips then did not have memory mapped IO, thus IO had a different
space. And on the AT you don't have to send a memory request through the
ISA bus all the time, I think it was only when using those XMS cards that
plugged on the ISA bus. Of course I could be wrong its been eans since I
played with them ATs.Also the early 386s by compaq were nothing more than
an AT with a 386. And they were not by any means cheap. I still remember
the days when a compaq 386sx would cost more than other vendors 486DX in
the days when the 486 was intels top chip. They just had a reputation for
durability. I think the only thing revolutionary that compaq did that
wasn't a copy were the early systempro machines that had symmetric
processors. Too bad that there were almost no OSs available for the PC
back then that could do anything with an extra processor. And I could be
also wrong but I recall that FLEX was used by ALR, but I think they used
it to name their architecture in which the processor sat on a
daughterboard, and you could upgrade it via a simple board swap. But this
was like a decade ago.... man I feel old.

