> Talking about hot - anyone with insight on the electronics side have an
> estimate of the power consumption of a 1 Ghz 21264 (I'm almost sure it's
> not "linear").
> 

My understanding of the physics is that each switching transition
dissipates some number of nanojoules.  Multiply that by Gigatransitions
per second and you get J/s = Watts.  Therefore, in fact, to a good 
approximation you *SHOULD* expect power dissipation to scale linearly
with frequency for any given physical chip.  The chips designed to
operate at a higher frequency are probably going to have design 
characteristics (operating voltage or physical dimensions) that reduce 
the nanojoules per transition, in order to ease the cooling problem,
so in practice the dissipation will increase less than in linear 
proportion to the frequency. 

