> >
> > > Now if you assume that Alpha scales linear to 1 GHz, too, then the
> > > Pentium-III looks not that hot even for integer.
> >
> > Talking about hot - anyone with insight on the electronics side have an
> > estimate of the power consumption of a 1 Ghz 21264 (I'm almost sure it's
> > not "linear").
> 
> It depends largely on the process being used. CMOS6 (unlikely to achieve
> 1GHz) would be a serious power pig; I believe the current EV6/600
> consumes 95W. Chances are that EV68 (CMOS8 process) will be the first to
> achieve 1GHz, and from what the CPQ engineers told me last September the
> power consumption would be "at or below" current levels. We'll see.

   This is quite believable and would track what happened with the shrink
   from the 21164 to 21164A

      CPU      Ld(um)    Freq (MHz)  Max Power (W)

     21164     0.50        250        43
     21164     0.50        300        51
     21164     0.50        333        56
     21164A    0.35        366        27.5
     21164A    0.35        400        30.5
     21164A    0.35        500        37.5
     21164A    0.35        600        45.0

  The same effect happened in x86 land going from Klamath (0.35 um) to
  Deschutes (0.25 um), the 333 MHz PII needed little over half the power
  of the 300 MHz PII.

  The deciding factor is how much the core supply voltage goes down by
  when the 21264 shrinks from 0.35 to 0.25 um.  The 21264 already runs
  at 2.2V so there might not be headroom to shrink it much further
  until 0.18 um.  If the core voltage doesn't scale down by at least
  the square root of the increase in clock frequency then the power
  will go up.  There is a small decrease in the internal equivalent
  switching capacitance when you shrink a design but the effect on
  power consumption is usually disappointingly second order compared
  to the effect of changes in clock frequency and supply voltage.

All opinions strictly my own.
--
