>They simply want a machine that doesn't need to be repaired very often at
>all. Like once in many years. I expect most of them are willing to accept a
>higher transient failure rate, so long as it is not too high, in return for
>less repairs.

I used to work for a company that made a medical printer.  We
distinguished two types of failures.  A "soft" failure -- typically a
jam -- could be repaired by the operator.  A "hard" failure was a
failure that required a service call, or three consecutive soft
failures.  The reliability goal was something like 1 or 2 service
visits per year for all reasons, including hard failures and scheduled
maintenance.


