   A company like Intel with its own fabs and its own motherboards
   has a whole bag of tricks it can pull in addition to supercooling
   to demo a 1 GHz Pentium III CPU.  They can specially screen out
   superfast parts from the extreme end of the process bell curve.
   They also have the option of a custom wafer run in which they
   lay down a dangerously thin gate oxide layer and overetch the poly
   critical dimensions for superfast transistors.  At the board level
   they can crank up the supply voltage to nearly destructive levels.

   Voila, a 1 GHz CPU to dazzle the gawking masses for a few hours.
   The trade press is too naive to care that the handpicked, supercooled
   chip would fail after a few hundred hours of such operation due to
   hot electron injection, dielectric breakdown, or electromigration.
   They just bark like trained seals and Intel gets its GigaHertz CPU
   headline.

> 
> Terry Shannon
> Who expects EV68 to come in early next year at 1GHz without cooling


