Greg Pfister (pfister@usNOSPAM.ibm.com) wrote:
: The report I saw defines a spectrum that associates each "number of 9s"
: level with a word phrase -- like "continuous availabiity," "fault
: tolerant," "highly available," "fault resiliant," "very highly
: available," etc. I don't have their list in front of me, but I seem to
: recall that "high availability" was one of the low-ish ones -- three 9s
: or so. Phrases like "very highly available," unsurprisingly, were
: applied to more reliable systems.

My original use was intended to be inexact but implying noticeably better
than 99%. When a reply was made that proposed an overly wide range of
numbers for the term, I whipped out the definition. I wouldn't have
bothered if we were arguing about class 3 vs. class 5.

There are two easy solutions to the meanigless hiearchy of words. Either
use availability class, which is the number of 9's in the number (or more
precisely log10(1 / (1 - A)) where A is a decimal percentage), or use the
number of minutes (or seconds) per year the system is allowed to be
unavailable (statistically speaking of course).

-Z-



