:>|> oxygen, so it kills humans much more effectively than Argon, which
:>|> merely sits there. And yes, Halon is an ozone-destroying gas, and so
:>|> rumor has it that you can't refill a halon system if it is discharged.
:>|> Argon, on the other hand, is 1% or so of the normal atmosphere.
:>|> 


  Halon does not bond with oxygen -- it blocks the chemical reaction 
  of combustion in ways not particularly well understood.  (Things
  bonding with oxygen are typically either rusting or burning. :-)


:>My understanding was that one of the attractive features of halon was that, in
:>addition to not wrecking the equipment, it was not harmful to people in low
:>concentrations necessary to extinguish fires (at least in the short to
:>intermediate term).  Do you have information to the contrary?  
:>
:>I figured it was banned like R12 due to adverse effects on the ozone.

  Variousa Halons have been banned.

:halon may not be directly harmful to people, but I remember from my computer
:room safety training a big emphasis on getting out quickly if the fire
:alarms went off, and not returning until the fire dept. cleared it.  The
:idea was clearly that a room rapidly filling with halon was not good for
:your health.  It may have just been that there was the potential for
:displacing too much oxygen which would mean you might pass out, or there
:might be risks of other fire-induced byproducts.  Anyway, don't let a
:feeling of 'halon is not bad for you' cause you to try for 'one last file
:save/edit' when the alarm sounds.  Exit immediately!  Losing a day's work is
:better than losing your life.

  The evacuation is for several reasons...

  Somebody may have miscalculated and designed the system to dump too 
  much Halon into the room.  (This is rare, a correctly calculated 
  room will have a survivable percentage of oxygen -- but computer
  room walls have been known to be moved around over the years.)  

  More importantly, the byproducts of "normal" combustion are particularly
  nasty compounds -- one commonly sees cyanide gas, as well as various 
  oxides of nitrogen.  Certain refrigerants produce phosgen gas -- this
  is a military-grade chemical weapon -- when burned.

  Some of these combustion products initially appear to be tolerated by 
  the body, yet can and have led to fatal pulmonary edema -- your lungs
  fill with your own fluid and you drown -- six or more hours after the
  exposure.

  I'd use self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) until the atmosphere 
  in the room has been sufficently vented, as well as the appropriate
  protective equipment.  If you're not trained and/or not equipped, get 
  out immediately.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




