I used to work at a company that had one of the worlds largest indoor
culimated radar range in the free world.  The building was about 130' H x
180' D x 100' W.  Most of which was open space.

To provide fire suppression would either require the equivalent of one
olympic size swimming pool of water being dumped into it every minute or
two 6' by 30' cylinders of halon.  (on a side-note they had to slow down
the dump a halon to prevent the roof from being blown off when it was
dumping and also to prevent the walls from being sucked in when the halon
expanded and cooled)

In the mid 80's this site did dump it's halon due to a faulty fire sensing
system (Not VMS based of course).  At that time the halon cost about
$750,000.  Needless to say that some people were rather upset.

However, since the ban on CFC et al. the value of the halon in these tanks
has now jumped up to over $120 million.  The people running the range were
curious when armed guards showed up one night to protect not the building
but the two halon tanks.

Not a bad return on your investment!

Cass Witkowski

P.S.  There was one man in the middle of the range during the release of
the halon.  He was up in a man cage on the crane.  When the halon went off
it's cooling effect dropped the temperature of the inside to the dew
point.  The entire range was filled with a dense fog.

Except for needing a new pair of underwear the man was unharmed.

"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:

>
> Interesting. Argon is an inert gas (same one used in lightbulbs, I
> believe). To the best of my knowledge Argon does not exhibit Halon's
> "chainbreaking" properties wherein the latter gas actually interrupts
> the combustion chain reaction.
>
> Depending on the fire load and the nature of the combustible material,
> Halon is effective in 3 percent to 8 percent concentrations, neither of
> which dilute the oxygen concentration below the 15 percent required to
> sustain combustion (or, for that matter, respiration). The stuff just
> puts out fires, not people. I surmise that an argon system relies on
> total flooding, which of course creates an atmosphere that will
> suffocate any personnel who have the misfortune of remaining in the
> protected area when the system dumps.
>
> I think it's a damned shame that Halon fire protection systems were
> outlawed by the Greens. Certainly Halon fire suppression systems
> contribute only a miniscule amount to atmospheric CFC levels. Air
> conditioners, refrigerators, et al are (or were) the real culprits in
> this regard.
