Greg Lindahl wrote:
> Gee, I've been working in machine rooms for 20 years with misleading
> warning signs. You learn something new every day.

I don't know the effect of Halon on humans, but I do know that
warning signs aren't a reliable source of detailed information.

The purpose of the warning sign is to "reduce harm".  Saying to
leave a possibly burning room because the fire extinguisher may
have bad effects accomplishes that goal even if the fire extinguisher
doesn't have bad effects.  Moreover, it can be expensive to
show that "some bad thing" wasn't the result of Halon exposure.

In other words, there's no down side to a sign which says that
Halon can kill and there's considerable upside, so don't expect
it to say anything different, regardless of what Halon actually does.

There are lots of legally or factually dubious signs whose
wording is influenced by legal reasons.  Consider the universal
"you agreed that we're not liable for damages when you accepted
this parking time slip" notice.   It's legally wrong.  However,
a lot of people believe it, so they don't press legitimate damage
claims.

-andy

