In article <36D0191A.3BA8@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
:Paul Repacholi wrote:
:> 
:> I noticed the other day that Curtin Unis main comp
:> centre has an Argon flood system to replace the Halon
:> one.
:> 
:> ~paul
:
:Interesting. Argon is an inert gas (same one used in lightbulbs, I
:believe). To the best of my knowledge Argon does not exhibit Halon's
:"chainbreaking" properties wherein the latter gas actually interrupts
:the combustion chain reaction.

  Argon total-flooding fire suppression systems operate by oxygen 
  displacement -- suffocation -- of the fire.  Unlike Halon 1301 
  (bromotrifluoromethane) and various similar halogenated compounds 
  used in fire suppression systems, argon does does not interfere 
  with the free radicals within the chemical combustion process.  
  Further -- unlike Halon 1301 -- argon is also not considered an 
  ozone-depletion risk, the noble gas that it is...

  I'd expect that an argon flooding system is designed to release argon 
  to dilute ambient oxygen to a concentration between the fatal range 
  and the lower limits of combustion -- this is one of the key benefits
  of halogenated systems, the required concentration of oxygen remaining
  can be higher.

:Depending on the fire load and the nature of the combustible material,
:Halon is effective in 3 percent to 8 percent concentrations, neither of
:which dilute the oxygen concentration below the 15 percent required to
:sustain combustion (or, for that matter, respiration). The stuff just
:puts out fires, not people. I surmise that an argon system relies on
:total flooding, which of course creates an atmosphere that will
:suffocate any personnel who have the misfortune of remaining in the
:protected area when the system dumps.

  Correct.  Further, only supplied-air or SCBA respirators will be
  effective within the flooded environment -- air filtration systems
  are, uh, inadvisable.  After the area is flooded, it can potentially
  cause suffocation to any occupants that inadvisably remain in the 
  flooded area.

  US OSHA requirements specify supplied-air or SCBA (self-contained 
  breathing apparatus) respirators for personel operating in an
  oxygen-deficient (below 19.5%) atmosphere.  (The oxygen-reduced
  atmosphere remaining after a Halon 1301 dump -- though engineered to 
  be survivable to the occupants -- would likely technically require 
  the use of supplied-air or SCBA respirator systems.)

  Combustion requires oxygen concentrations of 15% or above (hence this
  is likely the concentration a fire suppression system based on Argon 
  flooding will target), and marked symptoms arise in occupants exposed 
  to concentrations of 16% and below. (The mentation -- judgement -- of
  occupants will start to suffer not particularly far below that 16% 
  concentration, too.)

:I think it's a damned shame that Halon fire protection systems were
:outlawed by the Greens. Certainly Halon fire suppression systems
:contribute only a miniscule amount to atmospheric CFC levels. Air
:conditioners, refrigerators, et al are (or were) the real culprits in
:this regard.

  This is a tradeoff between the perceived risks of permanent sunburn,
  the benefits of Halon 1301 and similar systems, and the alternatives.
  There is active research for alternate "ozone friendly" fire suppression 
  systems going on.  At least one florine-based replacement for Halon 1301 
  -- FM-200 -- is reportedly available, has passed Underwriters Laboratories 
  and Factory Mutual testing, and is also "ozone-friendly".

:Terry Shannon
:Fire Protection System Designer in his pre-industry analyst days

  Hoff

