I might as well add my 2�.

The Volkswagen "Beetle" engine block (frame) and cylinders
are aluminum alloy.  It was the transmission casing that
was magnesium alloy.  According to one reference I've read,
at one time it was the largest single industrial use of
Magnesium alloy.  (The cylinders might be steel, but I'm
reasonably certain they are aluminum alloy with iron or
steel sleeves pressed in.)

I don't think magnesium fires are a big concern in computer
sites.  It's true that the arms that hold disk heads are often
magnesium alloy; but by the time the fire gets to one of those
you have much more serious things to worry about.  As Hoff
mentioned, the poisonous gasses given off when most plastics
and other synthetics burn are a much bigger worry.  Ditto for
a magnesium-framed laptop: by the time you ignite a solid
hunk of magnesium most of the rest of the laptop (like the
plastic case) will already be burning.

When we ignited magnesium metal strips in chemistry classes,
it took a lot more than a match: usually a bunsen burner was
needed to get it going.

The general topic of fire control is one which needs a lot
more attention, particularly in the U.S.  It appears that we
lag behind most of the rest of the industrealized world in
fire control in businesses, and particularly in homes, to
judge by the statistics.

When was the last time any of you had a fire safety review
in your site?  Is your computer room protected against fire?
Do you know where the exits are?

