Organization: The World Public Access UNIX, Brookline, MA
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch

Magnesium will burn spectacularly once ignited, and the ease to do so is
greatly dependent on the size of the pieces.  Aluminum is much the same way
except it forms a more resistant oxide layer.  The "salutes" (very loud
explosions with a white flash) at fireworks shows is just extremely fine
powdered aluminum with an oxygen-releasing chemical.  VW Beetle engine
blocks (original beetle) are of an alloy that's mostly magnesium, and
you can put well over 100,000 miles on that lump of Mg and it still won't
light.

-Mike



