Stefan Monnier <monnier+comp/arch/news/@tequila.cs.yale.edu> writes:

[On the performance of PCI]

>But it's not necessarily harmful either, which is what you seemed to claim
>earlier.  Of course, maybe it is harmful, I can't tell before seeing real
>(non-micro) benchmark results.

It depends critically on the implementation of the PCI bus and the cards
involved.  I've seen >100MB/s sustained throughput, but that was special
circumstances.  I'd look for 60-90MB/s generally.

One thing people forget about PCI, however, is that it can be made
faster- going from 32 bits wide to 64, or doubling the clock rate from
33MHz to 66MHz.  Doing both of these gives you a bus with a theoretical
rate of 528MB/s, and a real rate of 240-360MB/s.

Brian




