Michael Moroney wrote:
> 
> Magnesium will burn spectacularly once ignited, and the ease to do so is
> greatly dependent on the size of the pieces.  Aluminum is much the same way
> except it forms a more resistant oxide layer.  The "salutes" (very loud
> explosions with a white flash) at fireworks shows is just extremely fine
> powdered aluminum with an oxygen-releasing chemical.  VW Beetle engine
> blocks (original beetle) are of an alloy that's mostly magnesium, and
> you can put well over 100,000 miles on that lump of Mg and it still won't
> light.

The block was steel, AFAIK, but the top was Mg.

There was a few other uses for Mg in the Beetle, enough so that it is
still at or near the top of the list for the most Mg in a car.

My father used to work at Hydro's Magnesium Research Center in Porsgrunn
around this time, trying to find more and better uses for Mg. They sold
enough to the VW factory that Norway was the only country in the world
with a positive exchange ratio with the VW factory.

(He's retired but I work for the same company, which among many other
things is the worlds largest exporter of magnesium, and the second
largest in aluminum. There's lots more information on our home page,
http://www.hydro.com.)

Anyway, except for computer cases and lightweight portables, this has
very little to do with comp.arch, so it might be time to terminate this
thread? :-)

Terje

