andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com (andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com) wrote:
: True, but then there is no one such thing as High Availability

The Gray and Reuter book defines High Availability as class 5 availability,
99.999% available or less than 53 minutes per year downtime.

: I think this is the confusion, there is simply a range of availability 
: that a customer may require from 0% to 100% to meet their uptime 
: requirments.

I don't believe this is true. Consumers for example do not think that way.
They simply want a machine that doesn't need to be repaired very often at
all. Like once in many years. I expect most of them are willing to accept a
higher transient failure rate, so long as it is not too high, in return for
less repairs. This is a direct tradeoff in that fault-tolerant machines are
very likely to "consume hardware" at a higher rate than non-fault-tolerant
ones. In return, you get guaranteed uptime, specified responses to external
calamaties, often a completely different approach to systems software,
higher prices, etc.

So in practice, you have people who specify machines of 99% availability
and above, guaranteed, and you have people who want something that doesn't
crash very often and requires little repair. In the second category, it is
common to use some fault tolerant techniques to handle common failures.
E.g. uninteruptable power supplies and disk mirroring applied to an NT
server. Cheap and handles some common set of failures. But you are unlikely
to find such systems competing on guaranteed uptime contracts.

