Consumers, as in PC consumers don't think like this I agree
they just want something thats reliable where reliable is 
a somewhat wishy washy concept. Consumers probably don't 
know what high availability is and probably also don't want 
desktop clusters :-)

IS departments on the other hand do know what high availabilty 
is and they are generally driven by their customers requirements.
These customers may insist on say no more than 2 hours unscheduled 
outage in a month, this translates into an availability figure 
which in turn translates into a system configuration.

> So in practice, you have people who specify machines of 99% availability
> and above, guaranteed, and you have people who want something that doesn't
> crash very often and requires little repair. In the second category, it is
> common to use some fault tolerant techniques to handle common failures.
> E.g. uninteruptable power supplies and disk mirroring applied to an NT
> server. Cheap and handles some common set of failures. But you are unlikely
> to find such systems competing on guaranteed uptime contracts.
> 

