Greg Lindahl wrote:
> 
> Del Cecchi <dcecchi@ibm.net> writes:
> 
> > Perhaps Halon is a greenhouse gas, and hence banned? Of course an argon
> > flood will kill any people, whereas Halon doesn't, being used in low
> > concentrations.  Or maybe it is holes in the ozone.
> 
> I don't know what planet you're from, but Halon bonds with all the
> oxygen, so it kills humans much more effectively than Argon, which
> merely sits there. And yes, Halon is an ozone-destroying gas, and so
> rumor has it that you can't refill a halon system if it is discharged.
> Argon, on the other hand, is 1% or so of the normal atmosphere.
> 

Last time I checked, my planetary origin was Earth. Halon in fact was
banned because it is a greenhouse gas: seems we signed some UN treaty
regarding greenhouse gases, and the fools who drafted the treaty
neglected to exempt fire protection systems. Duhhh...

Argon and CO2 and the like suppress combustion by displacing oxygen.
Accordingly, a concentration of at least 30 percent is needed to dilute
the oxygen content of a protected area from ~20 percent to below the ~15
percent necessary to sustain combustion.

The Halons suppress combustion by inhibiting the production of free
radicals which are necessary to support the the combustion chain
reaction. The design criteria for a Halon system usually calls for a 7
or 8 percent concentration of the gas when the system is discharged
(concentrations of as little as 3 percent will extinguish many common
fires). As a result, the oxygen concentration in a protected area will
drop to perhaps ~18 percent when a system is discharged. Combustion is
not supported in such an atmosphere, but it has negligible impact on
one's ability to breathe.

When Halon systems were first deployed, the initial certification test
involved triggering and dumping the system in the protected area. This
wasted a lot of Halon. Wiser minds prevailed, and certification tests
were conducted with nitrogen or argon (with, of course, no personnel in
the protected area). Once the test was completed, the system was
recharged with Halon.

Terry Shannon





