Terje Mathisen wrote:
> 
> 
> Re. the CFC levels, isn't one of the problems with Halons the fact that
> at least some of them can break down into highly toxic/corrosive
> compounds when exposed to open flames?

Yup. Ever hear of phosgene gas? The Germans made use of this during the
WWI trench fights. Carbon tetracholoride (a cleaning fluid and
halogenated hydrocarbon in its own right) decomposes into phosgene when
sufficiently heated; other Halons produce similar Bad Compounds.

In the case of Halon fire suppression agents, chances are good that they
will extinguish a fire in its incipient or very early stages, thus the
temperature in a protected area will not have time to get hot enough to
transform Halon into products of decomposition. 

What's more, the threshold of toxicity for Halon decomposition products
is well above the threshold of detectibility... you will SMELL the stuff
at concentrations well below harmful levels. And the stuff smells VERY
bad so you probably won't hang around in the area in question.

As for carbon tetrachloride, which was used in fire extinguishers here
in the USA some 60 years ago, such use was banned after a few people
died from inadvertantly gassing themselves with phosgene.

Terry Shannon




