Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com (andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com) wrote:
> : True, but then there is no one such thing as High Availability
> 
> The Gray and Reuter book defines High Availability as class 5 availability,
> 99.999% available or less than 53 minutes per year downtime.

However, there are equally authoritative sources, like IEEE committees
on the subject, that don't use this definition. They have published
reports / manuals / whatever, the results of cross-industry concensus, I
guess (or at least concensus among those who attended the meetings).

The report I saw defines a spectrum that associates each "number of 9s"
level with a word phrase -- like "continuous availabiity," "fault
tolerant," "highly available," "fault resiliant," "very highly
available," etc. I don't have their list in front of me, but I seem to
recall that "high availability" was one of the low-ish ones -- three 9s
or so. Phrases like "very highly available," unsurprisingly, were
applied to more reliable systems.

I personally think the IEEE scheme is not terribly useful, since the
phrases chosen don't have any natural hierarchy to me.

I also think any such descriptive phrases are pretty much useless, since
they are too loaded with potential meaning to a customer, and so will
get used pretty much at random in the press (fed by marketing / PR) and
in the field -- they're just trying to make sure the customer hears what
he/she wants to hear.

This is unfortunate. It would be nice to have consistent industry
technology.

This is also inevitable, unless English gets some "word police" (like
French has). But that cure is likely to be worse than the disease.

Greg Pfister
