Do you still use water in your machine rooms ? They 
arn't showers you know :-)


> Absolutely, someone made the perfectly valid statement that
> redundancy _can_  ( and often does) reduce reliability. Obviously
> well designed redundancy that protects the _most_ frequent failure
> modes will greatly improve reliability. But there is always some
> penalty.
> 

Sure, badly designed redundancy can be a big minus
determining what constitutes failure in failfast 
systems can be hard. 

But this is not an argument for not doing any 
redundancy at all.


>   Yes they do! In well designed systems the number of times they save you
> is greater than the number of times they kill you.
> 
>   My wife just had a lot of grief with a computer that wouldn't stay up. It runs
> the library she works at.  Problem? The UPS was failing.

But this may not be a reason not to have a UPS, this is after 
all the whole point of the discussion. Well designed redundancy 
can reduce the risk of failure. Your wife may get more failures 
with no UPS if the power to the building is less reliable 
than the UFS. The library she works for could also invest in 
a more reliable UPS. 

