>> >Anyone know the ignition point for magnesium?
>...
>>  About 650� C for massive magnesium...
>> ... the original proposition was that you "didn't
>> know of anything that burns in CO2". :-)

Any open flame will ignite powder or ball-milled flakes
of magnesium quite easily. 

>Titanium also burns in both CO2 and in N2, but the ignition
>point is about 3000C (think "welding torch" :-).

Titanium will also ignite at lower temps; two jobs ago, we
used TI flakes or filings to get gold sparks out of the
pyrotechnics we manufactured. It was just mixed in with a
black powder/binder/stearic acid "dough" which we then 
pressed into cardboard cylinders to make gerbs. (Imagine
a model rocket motor upside down that spews a nice spray
of sparks when ignited; color of the spark depended upon
the type of metal flakes used.) We also used fine flakes
in overhead starburst-type devices. As I recall, most of
the stuff would ignite at about 350F, and burned somewhat
hotter. But, you can take a bit of powder, light a lighter,
and trickle the powder into it a bit at a time, and most
of it will ignite.

I've heard of one incident in which a piece of magnesium 
was ignited by an idiot grinding it.

I have no idea what temps it would take to ignite something
like one of my VAX 6200s, but in addition to temperature,
you have to have enough heat going into it. Big pieces are
much harder to light off, and even harder to extinguish.
For magnesium, water is an oxidizer...


Gary

