Terry C. Shannon (shannon@world.std.com) wrote:
: As for magnesium, flammability is directly related to granularity.
: Finely divided particles or shavings are easy to ignite; it takes
: considerably more effort to ignite the case of a HiNote Ultra notebook.

Just about *any* metal (with the possible exception of the coinage
metals) will burn merrily if dispersed finely enough. For example,
the very fine iron powder obtained by thermal decomposition of
iron oxalate has been known to ignite spontaneously in the air.
Similarly, a very fine copper powder will react with water (displacing
hydrogen), even though bulk copper is inert to water. Chemistry of
finely dispersed materials can be *really* fascinating...

Regards,

/Serge.P



