But did the article discuss the merits of having no redundancy 
vs having some redundancy. You seem to be arguing that too 
much redundancy is a bad thing, I think that is fair and 
having say 7 way redundancy probably does introduce so much 
complexity that it is pointless. 

This however does not seem like an argument that supports 
no redundancy at all. 


> My point was that there are many ways to design for reliability, especially
> when cost is a part of the design goals.  Customers care more about results
> than methods.  I might think that critizing your competitor's methods
> instead of emphasizing your own strengths suggests you don't want to compare
> results.
