No, it think there is ample evidence that the Sun and HP approach
deliver 
very different benefits. This is probably best illustrated by a quote 
from your posting "These sorts of customers demand fail-over
capability".

Customers don't generally demand failover, they demand availability,
this is not necessarly delivered by going to a failover solution.

Failover also fails your own complexity test dramatically. Lets 
use the following very simple example. A customer has two systems 
one is a Compaq proliant one is a H-P V-Class, note no Sun's in this 
example. Both machines have lost a PSU, this is not a problem they 
both have redundant power, note they also have a simularly complex 
design to support this redundancy. 

Now we need to replace this PSU, on the HP this is impossible without 
shutting the machine down, what is the remedy for this, well 
its the one you mention clustering. 

The Compaq on the otherhand can do this online without a cluster 
because it has hot-pluggable PSU's. This BTW is at the cost of 
minimal extra complexity. On the other hand the V-Class cluster 
is an enormous extra complexity, there is a whole additional machine, 
Serviceguard, shared storage, etc etc.

So where does it leave us, sadly you seem to have shot your 
remaining foot off, since by not adding basic redundancy to 
the V-Class platform you force customers to cluster to 
address these very basic redundancy issues in the process 
vastly increasing the complexity of the whole solution.


> Just for the record, the V-class design was led by a design team mostly
