> was not started until after HP purchased Convex.  The design for the S- and
> X- class machines were started before HP purchased Convex.  From an external
> programming model point of view, the V-class is similar to the S-class, but
> from an internal point of view contains many newer gate arrays and other
> design improvements.
> 

Just for the record what are the actual differences in the design of 
the S-Class and the V-Class, their specs are alomost identical and 
as you know the S-Class pre dates the V-Class. 


> HP has sold V-class machines to customers with extreme reliability
> requirements.  These sorts of customers demand fail-over capability.  HP has
> also sold V-class machines to commerical customers who do not have extreme
> availability requirements.  Those customers don't want to pay for extra
> redundancy features.  I'm not a high-availability expert, so I have not
> tried to go into the details of how and why the HP solutions works.  I just
> know that demanding customers come back and buy again.
> 

Now we get to the quick of it. Customers don't demand 
failover solutions unless they have been told thats what they 
want. Customers demand uptime, its up to the platform vendor
to fit the customers uptime requirement into a platform 
configuration. 

If a customer can have a single system with redundancy in 
the box that meets their uptime requirements then why 
would they want to go to the additional expense of having 
two or more machines clustered. 

Customers with very high levels of uptime requirements
may find that clusters are the only solution. But dont
force them to cluster at lower uptime levels simply 
to be able to on-line service a fan or PSU.


Andrew Harrison
