Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> Chris (icq@dial.pipex.com) wrote:
> : I think you're missing the point - The reduction in mtbf is not because
> : of the redundant components, but because of the extra hardware required
> : to to interconnect them and switch out the failure.
> 
> This is basically not true. Most failure models give reduced or barely
> increased reliability through redundancy *unless* you have repair.
> 

I must be missing something here - I'm not arguing with your thesis, not
having read the books, though such research efforts often seem to
confirm what common sense would suggest.

Most redundancy schemes require extra hardware to support the redundancy
- it's this that degrades the overall mtbf since it's common to _all_
the redundant paths. And yes, it can itself be duplicated, but at some
point etc etc.

The hardware mtbf matrix _has_ changed since the 70's. Systems were
built with bigger boards, more heat, much more ssi and discreet
components, disk drives and memory were less reliable etc. Any study
done then on the basis of individual component type mtbf would need to
be re-evaluated for current reliability figures.

Not trying to nitpick...

Rgrds, Chris



