Libor wrote in message ...

>"Extinguishing magnesium fires presents special problems.
>Because magnesium combines readily with oxygen, water
>applied to the fire may decompose into oxygen and hydrogen,
>allowing the oxygen to combine with the magnesium and the
>hydrogen to intensify the fire. None of the common gaseous
>extinguishing agents (for example, CO2) are suitable. Magnesium,
>which will burn in an atmosphere of CO2 , will also burn in nitrogen,
>forming magnesium nitride. The use of water, water solutions,
>or inert gases are not effective on magnesium chip fires. Halon
>extinguishing agents react violently with burning magnesium." [1]

I wonder if you can get argon extinguishers for dealing with really
reactive fires?

(covering with sand is out, too - 4Mg + SiO2 -> Mg2Si + 2MgO, and the
magnesium silicide then reacts with water to produce SiH4, which (apart
from its use in semiconductor manufacture) explodes in presence of air
or water)

Tom




