You can easily ignite magnesium powder or magnesium strips with a match.
It is a bit more difficult to light up solid magnesium, but once the
fire started it certainly keeps going ;-0) Many other metals will also 
burn in CO2 and nitrogen, including some of those which are supposed to 
be pretty inert - e.g. titanium. I wouldn't be surprized if some of the 
more tough alimunium allows (which may contain ca. 10% of lithium) will 
burn quite well in CO2, too.

You should come and see a machining workshop dealing with magnesium
and titanium one day ... or better still, listen to the horror stories
of inductrial firefighters assigned to one.

