> Definitely George Goble, there's a warning to that effect on the
> web-page. Except that he claims that *one briquette* presoaked in LOX
> is approximately equvivalent to 1 stick of dynamite.

  This is quite credible.

  In first year engineering my first class on Friday morning was a chemistry
  lecture which I never missed because my prof, an otherwise stuffy fellow
  who talked like he should host masterpiece theatre, liked to demo anything
  pyrotechnic.

  One morning he had a small piece of stale graham cracker about 1 cm2 and
  soaked it in a beaker of LO2.  He meant to just give it a quick dip and
  then ignite it with a bunson burner for a bright flash.  Unfortunately
  it slipped out of the tongs and he took several minutes to fish it out
  of the LO2.  He went ahead and put it in the flame...

  There was a most satisfyingly earsplitting detonation which blew the
  prof's glasses off his face and bent the tongs out to 90 deg or so.  His
  only words were a sputtering "this didn't happen last time".  Fortunately
  no one was hurt but my ears were ringing (i was at the *back* of the
  lecture hall) for the rest of the morning.

  As far as magnesium goes, either magnesium or aluminum in fine powder
  form is higly explosive if dispersed in air.  IBM had a disk drive plant
  in California blow up in the 70's or possibly early 80's from airborne
  metallic dust igniting.

> 
> That would make a full charcoal bed quite a bit more powerfull.

