In article <36CABF12.654A348D@lmco.com>, Bill Fry <william.h.fry@lmco.com> writes:
:I have a Microvax II which will not complete booting because of a disk
:failure.  The system disk is OK, and I get to the site specific boot
:commands and it tries to mount the failed (user) disk and hangs waiting
:for an operator to mount the requested volume.
:
:My question is, how can I bypass or interrupt the boot process at a 
:point where I can edit the startup file and comment out the mounting of
:the failed disk.

  Request a conversational bootstrap (assuming dua0:) using R5,
  disable saving the changed parameter value by clearing the
  WRITESYSPARAMS

  >>> b/r5:1 dua0
  SYSBOOT> SET STARTUP_P1 "MIN"
  SYSBOOT> SET WRITESYSPARAMS 0
  SYSBOOT> CONTINUE

  You will then see an abbreviated ("minimal") system bootstrap, 
  running only the core of the system startup procedures.  You will 
  be able to log in (only) from the console, and can then (hopefully) 
  repair the problem in SYSTARTUP*.COM.

  comp.os.vms is a better spot for OpenVMS questions, and please
  indicate the OpenVMS version when posting.  (As you may well be 
  aware, the name of the site-specific startup command procedure 
  does vary by the OpenVMS version.)

  Also see the system management documentation -- information on
  this topic is included in the startup and shutdown information.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




