Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com (andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com) wrote:
> : But did the article discuss the merits of having no redundancy
> : vs having some redundancy.
> : This however does not seem like an argument that supports
> : no redundancy at all.
> 
> If you are not going to have repair and do not need high availability,
> there are very good arguments to be made for having little or no
> redundancy. (You may still need a little to cover things which have a high
> transient error rate.) Or in more general terms, reducing the number of
> components in the system to the bare minimum.
> 

True, but then there is no one such thing as High Availability
I think this is the confusion, there is simply a range of availability 
that a customer may require from 0% to 100% to meet their uptime 
requirments. Of course no one wants 0% availability and most people 
would like close to 100% availability but this costs money. 

So instead you get systems configured individually to meet a specified 
level of uptime. I have selected some hypothetical numbers.

99%	Disk mirroring or duplexing
99.5%	Ditto plus redundant power, cooling
99.95%	Ditto plus hot pluggable I/O, CPU's and memory, redundant power
	input.
99.99%	Cluster 
99.999%	FT lockstep system.

(this is not necessarely how Sun would configure these systems, 
its just an illustration)

Overlayed on these systems may then be a requirement for DR, which 
may have little relationship to the availability requirments of the 
systems. 

> If you need high availability, there is simply no substitute for redundancy
> and repair.
> 
> [A lot of "imagine this and imagine that" with no point I can discern.]
> 
> : If a customer can have a single system with redundancy in
> : the box that meets their uptime requirements then why
> : would they want to go to the additional expense of having
> : two or more machines clustered.
> 
> Because in theory you can run each element of the cluster in a different
> geographical location to CYA against really bad stuff. Though if you are
> going this far, you probably want to cluster systems that already have very
> high availability features.
> 

Sure, this is an advantage of a cluster, it can provide 
you with a DR capability. This however does not address 
the issues surrounding the availability of each node in 
the cluster. 

Ideally you will never need to use your cluster for 
failover or your DR system because you don't get the 
event that requires DR and your nodes that make up 
the cluster prove to be highly reliable. It is however 
your insurance policy.

> : Customers with very high levels of uptime requirements
> : may find that clusters are the only solution. But dont
> : force them to cluster at lower uptime levels simply
> : to be able to on-line service a fan or PSU.
> 
> So now you are saying that clustering is the solution for really high end
> high availability requirements? I can sort of see the argument, but I'd
> like it to be much more quatitative before I draw any conclusions.
> 

Well you could read it that way but it also depends on how you 
calculate availability.

Look at two different measures, availability as measured by uptime 
against unscheduled outages or availability as measured by 
uptime against all outages, scheduled and unscheduled. 

In the case of the HP V-Class vs Compaq Proliant example, lets 
assume that both systems are 100% reliable except their PSU's 
and FANs. In the case of a PSU failure they are both able to 
acheive the same level of uptime if measured against unscheduled 
outages since they both will survive the unscheduled failure 
of a PSU.

Compaq can replace that PSU online, HP need to replace it by 
shutting the machine down, but this is a scheduled outage 
and therefore if you only measure unscheduled outages it 
does not count. Now this sort of configuration is fine for 
people who have reasonable windows during a 24 hour period 
or week where this outage can be scheduled. It is very 
much less acceptable to people who operate 24 by 7 operations.

People who run 24 by 7 should be much more interested in 
the combined scheduled and unscheduled downtime and this 
is an example of where you may need a cluster. 

> If you are already forced to have multiple data centers with hot fail over
> capability, it might be cost effective to use cheaper machines in the data
> centers. I expect it depends on the details of the failure analysis.
> 
> Since you are in IT marketing at Sun, perhaps you could post a URL to Sun's
> published reliability information for the E10k systems. I don't expect
> there to be a single MTTF for the e10k, but I would expect a document
> giving a lot of data that one can use to establish MTTFs in various
> circumstances. Or is that all NDA type stuff?
> 

I am not in IT marketing, I am an IT architect posted by Sun to 
a large bank to advise them on their IT infrastructure. One 
project we are working on does require guaranteed levels 
of availability. Prior to architecting if that is the 
right term, I was responsible for designing and writing 
Sun UK's HA cluster product (this pre dated Sun having a 
cluster product worldwide). I am very familiar with the 
practicality of designing HA systems and the issues 
surrounding failure detection. 

As for the MTBF of an E10K, you are really asking me 
to tell you how long a piece of string is. You should 
know that MTBF numbers are dependent on the configuration. 

An E10K with an empty cabinet will have a huge MTBF, 
a fully configured machine will have a lower MTBF. 

It is as far as I can remember NDA material but 
any Sun customer can ask for and get the MTBF 
of their systems. Given the influence of configuration 
on overal MTBFs I am dubious about releasing the 
numbers except as some sort of Marketing program. 
But then you would get Sun HP and IBM slugging 
it out over whose MTBFS were bigger.


Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



