In article <F7EI38.Gq7@spcuna.spc.edu>, Terry Kennedy <terry@spcunb.spc.edu> writes:
:Philipp Buehler <pb@xlink108.xlink.net> writes:
:> external CD-ROM from DEC labeled 'RRD40' .. external connector is
:> D-SUB15, internal I can see a 15 pol and a 10pol on the drive itself.
:> The 10pol is _not_ connected. The drive uses small caddies.
:>                                                            
:> Any clue? It was possible on !some! 3100 .. nothing more known about it.
:
:  It uses a LMSI proprietary interface. DEC had controllers for the IAS bus
:(KRP50) and Q-bus (presumably KRQ50). There was also a LMSI SCSI adapter for
:these drives.

  There were a couple of interfaces available for the RRD40 -- SCSI was 
  one, the other was "pseudo LMSI".  The SCSI version has a widget that 
  converted SCSI into the "pseudo LMSI" protocol.  The drive might or 
  might not work with an LMSI interface -- I don't know for sure.

  The 15-pin connector here very likely indicates this is the "pseudo
  LMSI" interface -- this was commonly used with the KRQ50 Q-bus widget.

:  It's a slow (1x or slower, due to really slow seeks) drive that uses funky
:caddys.

  Having just waited for an RRD40 to load OpenVMS onto a system, I 
  can confirm that is a very slow CD-ROM drive by present standards.
  The RRD40 would not serve as particularly effective gyroscope. :-)

  There were several different caddies used early on in CD-ROM drives,
  the "sleeve" or "antlers" caddy used in the RRD40 was actually quite
  common at the time -- you could pick up five-packs at the local Radio 
  Shack...  (I still have a couple of these caddies rattling around my
  office.)  Caddyless CD-ROM drives have obviously become the standard.

  I'd look around for an RRD43 or later CD-ROM drive, personally.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




