Not to mention handling the plethora of compilation models and libraries
most systems now support.  All of the issues such as debug/non-debug,
single-threaded/multi-threaded, sizes of fundamental types, etc. should be
compiled into the object modules. If at link time, inconsistencies are
present, they would be signaled instead of just producing an executable
that doesn't work. (This has the added benefit of being able to produce a
single library that handles all the cases instead of requiring the poor
programmer to pick which of eight different libraries is the right one.)

(E.g. I recently got screwed by code not compiled with the appropriate
preprocessor defines for multi-threading. So it was looking for errno in
the wrong place. The problem arose because the code I was compiling got
dynamically loaded into another executable which was not compiled to be
thread reentrant. I would have been much happier if the dynamic load flamed
out saying "Could not resolve symbol _mt_errno" or some such.)

