Maynard Handley (handleym@ricochet.net) wrote:
: You seem to mean by ABI a much larger OS-type API. 

In the UNIX realm, and ABI (Application Binary Interface) typically
includes "system calls" as well as the calling convention and executable
format. These are specified as being in a dynamically bound library the
application (or driver) binds to using the executable format mechanisms
specified in a lower level of the ABI.

In the case of device drivers, this list of dynamically bound routines
provides utillities and services a device driver needs to communicate with
hardware, communciate iwht OS facillities, etc. So in theory, the ABI
defines "everything" you need to ship one set of bytes that works on a
given hardware platform regardless of the operating system. (Whether that
bucket of bytes is an application, driver, plug-in, or library...)

This is almost possible and definitely useful but not without
compromise. (As usual, we can postulate arbitrarily small amounts of
compromise with arbitrarily large amounts of competence and imagination
applied to the problem.)

Even if we are not going to dictate the system call or driver call
interfaces, agreeing on a calling convention and an executable format is an
incredible win. (And one which is enjoyed by the Open Source movement as
much as anyone else. I'm sure the compiler developers would be happier if
every platform standardized on ELF or some such than having to support
umpteen incompatible implementations of the same conceptual thing...)

-Z-



