> I'm not sure what isqrt means; candidates would be integer square
> root and reciprocal square root.  The 21264 has neither AFAIK but

Sorry, isqrt is just my short name for inverse (i.e. reciprocal!) sqrt.

> it does haves direct moves between integer and floating point
> registers and an FP hardware divider that does something like six
> bits/cycle and can run in parallel with the hardware square root
> unit, so both candidates, while not directly present, can proceed
> with enhanced throughput on a 21264.
> 
> >16 cycles for single is quite nice. :-)
> >
> >(Compared to 70 on Pentium+, which always calculates 80-bit results)
> 
> I thought PentiumPro chips calculate fsqrt in 28, 57, or 68 clocks
> depending on precision (AP-526).  It seems to me like a failure of

Thanks, I never manged to find this piece of info in the original PPro
documentation, i.e. the yellow NDA books.

> compiler technology that these hardware fsqrt instructions are really

> needed: A Pentium classic can attain over twice the floating point
> fsqrt throughput using its floating multiplier (at least for double
> precision arguments) and even a lowly 21164 can achieve 16 cycle
> throughput for double precision square roots if we aren't worried
> about an ulp or two roundoff error in the results.  Both results

This is the real culprit: SQRT is the most complicated operation with
exact IEEE semantics, it is indeed fairly easy to speed it up if you can
allow less than perfect results.

> could be faster if a lookup table were allowed. The compiler must
> spot opportunities to parallelize the square roots to reach these
> throughput levels, though, and current compilers rarely seem to
> take advantage of much potential parallelism at all.

I wrote code a couple of years ago to help a guy in sweden, doing
molecular simulations (7-day runs on Alpha, longer on PII, but not too
long due to hw sqrt):

The critical operation was the reciprocal distance, which had to be
calculated as the reciprocal square root of the squared (x,y,z) deltas.

Using a table lookup to kickstart the first round of an NR iteration,
and calculating 3 of them in parallel (matching the 3-atom H2O
molecules), the resulting simulation code ran almost exactly twice as
fast, i.e. 3.5 days instead of 7.

The minor ulp errors were too small to give any difference in a test run
of the simulation, all parameters were equal to the old results, when
printed with 10 decimal digits.

This is still somthing that a compiler cannot do on its own, not without
getting explicit permission by the user.

Terje

