You have a conspiracy complex.

PEF was not invented with the intention of being proprietary. It was
invented because XCOFFs are too darn big for a personal computer,
especially one where virtual memory is a user option and a single address
space is used.

FYI, Mac OS will happily run an XCOFF (and older versions of Lightsoft's
Fantasm development environment generated application XCOFFs).

The PEF format internals are completely documented at
<http://developer.apple.com/techpubs/mac/runtimehtml/RTArch-2.html>. The
matching header file, PEFBinaryFormat.h is part of the standard Universal
Headers distribution (also available online as well as with all Mac OS
compilers). I don't see anything missing from these documents;
PEFBinaryFormat.h is the header used for all of Apple's own PEF-related
tools (the Code Fragment Manager, PPCLink, MakePEF, DumpPEF, etc.).

Yes, portions of PEF are covered by patents. Since nobody else has
bothered with this sort of compression in binary formats, it one of the
few software patents I think was deserved. Last I checked, Apple wasn't
charging for licenses to these patents. (You may have noticed that BeOS
used PEF.)

