Alberto Moreira (junkmail@moreira.mv.com) wrote:
: So, one trend out there is to bypass the whole thing, OS and all.
: Where would ABI stand at that light ? 

The ABI is still there for most of the stuff an app has to do that is not
rendering. (E.g. are you going to talk to the disk hardware directly?)

I have not read the paper you are talking about, but I would guess that it
proposes to standardize some level of hardware interface. Which is the
moral equivalent of an ABI at some level. In fact, it is unlikely that the
top level software model will change all that much. One can imagine the
compiler knowing things about rendering and optimizing appropriately, but
we'll probably still be writing things that look like function calls for a
lot of the rendering operations.

These "things that look like funtion calls" might compile into loads and
stores instead of calls to a library routine which either does loads and
stores or calls a device driver. If we work a little harder, we can delay
the decision about what to compile our rendering program into until it is
actually bound to a specific piece of hardware. (Which comes down to
of just in time compilation for a rendering language, perhaps one embedded
in a more general purpose programming language.)

But no matter what, the reality is you absolutely have to let people write
programs which outlive current hardware implementations. If you don't
you're technology is unlikely to be very successful.

-Z-



