>present ones, or both?  Obviously, I'm not interested in a server but a
>workstation (at least as long as a server costs substantially more).
>What machines would you recommend to do what I want to do?


From my personal experience I would say that most Alpha-based computers
currently available will run Linux, OpenVMS, Windows NT and Digital Unix,
with the exception perhaps of the older Alpha systems.  However, you would
currently be safe in acquiring a Digital Personal Workstation (DPW), either
the a or au series.  You might contemplate an Alpha built upon one of the
AT/ATX motherboards such as the PC164LX.  Many of the third party Alpha
manufacturers sell workstations based on these motherboards.  Some even sell
them with VMS, though most sell them with the other three operating systems
or even just NT.  They are cheaper, but definitely for good reason.  Their
performance are not as good as the DPWs.  Also, the older servers and
workstations are good bargains.  We recently bought a few AlphaServer 300's
and 400's.  They run VMS and NT (and probably the others).  They work great,
if not nearly as fast as the newer systems, but the price is right.

>My second question concerns Windows(NT).  Assuming we're all running the
>same (latest) versions of the software in question, is WindowsNT on
>Alpha fully compatible with Windows95 or whatever the current version is
>on Intel hardware, as far as Microsoft Word documents are concerned?
>For my purposes, I need 100% compatibility: my colleagues should not
>know or care what hardware I'm running on, and I don't want to have to
>fiddle with anything.


There are two current possibilities.  The one I prefer is to install the
Alpha native version of Word 97.  It can read and write Microsoft Word 97
documents (and other formats) just fine (otherwise what good would it be?).
The other possibility is to install FX!32 on your system and then install
the Intel version of Word 97.  This works, but it definitely is a tad
sluggish.  Though in a word processor, one only notices how slow it is
during certain operations.

>I guess another possibility would be Word for VMS or some sort of Intel
>hardware emulator (I've seen these running on IBM Unix boxes and even
>Word documents being produced there).  Do such beasts exist?  If so,
>would the 100% compatibility I need be possible?  If this is a viable
>way to go, it seems that this is what I would prefer.


Never heard of such a thing.  I know there use to be a PC-emulator available
for VMS or something of that nature.  It is available from DEC, but I don't
think it can handle anything beyond simple DOS-based applications.  It might
be able to run Windows 3.1, but I sincerely doubt that it could run Windows
95/98/NT.  Microsoft did make Word 6.0 for DOS.  However I don't think that
qualifies as a simple DOS application and it may be difficult to obtain that
old program.

Wayne Rigby
metahawk@earthling.net






