Jan Vorbrueggen (jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de) wrote:
: patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii) writes:

: > ... which is fine, except that by running a statically linked DU binary
: > on a Linux machine not licensed to run DU you are in a technical violation
: > of the terms of your DU licence and Compaq copyrights.

: Why should that be the case? Since when has selling compiler/linker output
: required selling a OS license to go with it as well?

Because if you statically link an executable, you include portions on
the libraries you link against, including any language-specific
libraries and general system libraries. The rights of use and distrbution
for this code are governed by copyright laws and by licensing agreements 
under which you obtained the libraries in the first place.

Since the only (legal) way of obtaining a right to use DU libraries 
is to buy a DU licence, running a statically linked executable on a
non-licensed machine is a violation of Decpaq's copyright. Naturally,
it will all change if they choose to sell their compiler (and requisite
libraries) for Linux, or if you'll negotiate a special licensing
agreement with Compaq.

Regards,

/Serge.P



