Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:

> Why should that be the case? Since when has selling compiler/linker output
> required selling a OS license to go with it as well?

Ah youth.  It was fairly common in the mini-computer and supermini-computer
eras (ie, late 70's and the 80's).  I know when I worked in the languages group
at Data General, it was a frequent complaint that when you bought a DG
language, you had to by a right to use license for the given language for any
machine you were going to run the binary on (which for many small OEMs,
etc. forced them into writing assembler, or not entering the DG market so that
they would have to require the severl thousand $ license).

Whether DEC realized that the era had passed (until they got bought by Compac)
and adjusted the licenses is a different question.  Since I haven't paid
attention to DEC's licensing practices for quite some time, I can't say.

