In article <36C7E8A5.41C67EA6@dial.pipex.com>,
Chris  <icq@dial.pipex.com> wrote:

>So, if i'm an apps developer, writing for DU, then I have to get a
>license to sell the finished product from Dec - for every copy ?. Can't
>believe this is what they intended.

Generally, the license of a shared library permits one to ship a program
that uses that shared library.  The user of the program will then be
subject to the license restrictions of the library found on the runtime
system (e.g. pay Compaq, or make the library source available according
to the LGPL).  With some object formats one can create a program that
uses a shared library without even having a copy of the shared library,
making a claim of copyright infringement difficult.  On AIX, for example,
the binder is happy to work with a text file containing the list of
symbols exported by the library.

Static libraries are different, and one may be prohibited from using
them on unlicensed systems.

For example, the Motif libraries were licensed under substantially
different terms depending on whether programs used static or dynamic
libraries.

(And you are using the wrong name for the operating system formerly
known as OSF/1.  Compaq found its missing 'u' and used it to build
the new OS name: "Tru64 UNIX".  Now please repeat after me 1000 times,
"Digital[R] UNIX is now Tru64[tm] UNIX[R].  VMS is now OpenVMS.  Bold
new names for the new millennium.  I must send Compaq money now.")

-- 
    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)



