patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii) writes:
> David Hinds (dhinds@zen.stanford.edu) wrote:
> : Some groups handle this by purchasing one or two Alpha systems to run
> : DEC UNIX and the DEC compilers, and a farm of Linux/Alpha boxes to do
> : computations: a static-linked DEC UNIX binary will run under Linux.
> 
> ... which is fine, except that by running a statically linked DU binary
> on a Linux machine not licensed to run DU you are in a technical violation
> of the terms of your DU licence and Compaq copyrights. [...]

I don't know about the license.  Can you point out what part would be
violated?

But I believe that copyright is a red-herring here.  Certainly it
would be illegal to copy dynamic-link libraries or library archives
verbatim.  But a program compiled with static linking is completely
different.  The resulting binary program contains only parts of some
library archives and they are no longer in archive format nor are the
archives recoverable.

Most importantly the binary program has significant new and original
input that was not present in the libraries, and copyright law allows
this.  While in many circumstance it is illegal to copy a photograph
belonging to somebody else, it is perfectly OK to make and publish an
artistic montage of a dozen copyrighted photographs, provided there is
significant new input.

If your claim was true, then even shipping dynamically linked programs
would be a violation of copyright since wrappers like crt0.o are
linked in!


However this area is certainly a "grey area".  I get the impression
that some people maintained the F.U.D. here when Alpha Linux clusters
became fashionable.  Presumably some marketing idiot figured that by
this kind of scare-tactics they could make a pretty penny by forcing a
few thousands of copies of Digital Unix onto people buying Alpha
workstations for clusters.  Instead they problaby succeeded in halving
the market for said workstations.

Compaq should root up this particular variety of nonsense and stamp it
out with clear statements.


The only result it has had on me is that I stopped developping for
Digital Unix.  Up to a year or so ago, I ensured that any software I
was writing worked equally well on Digital Unix and Alpha Linux.
After hearing more and more rumours like in Serguei's post, I stopped
(hey, F.U.D. works).  So now my code runs like a champ on Alpha Linux
but I've no idea whether it runs at all on Digital Unix.  It probably
wouldn't require much porting but I'm not about to do it.

So if you want to calculate with billion-bit numbers using the next
version of GMP, you'd better get Linux on your Alpha.

Bye,
  Rob.



