In article <7a4s3f$5sp$1@probity.mcc.ac.uk>, shouldn't.know@the.office writes:
:...at least some Alphas will run both VMS and WindowsNT.  

  Correct.

:Note that I don't necessarily need the possibility of running them 
:simultaneously (probably wouldn't work) or even alternately; rather, 
:after I'm finished with Word, I would like to install VMS on the box.

  You can shut down Windows NT, toggle console environment settings,
  and reboot OpenVMS.  Each operating system will need its own disk(s).

:Basically, I have two questions.  First, I seem to gather from the 
:discussions in the group, and from Terry Shannon's pronouncements, that 
:in the future not only will non-VMS workstations not be able to run VMS 
:at all, but the "VMSstation" or whatever will not run other operating 
:systems.  

  I'm not in a position to discuss specific details of officially 
  unannounced systems -- I am also not in a position to discuss the
  validity (or the invalidity) of any of Terry's statements.  (I'd
  like to, but you might see see my postings here end here rather
  quickly thereafter. :-)

  Baseline OpenVMS support for the OpenVMS workstation is presently
  expected for (if memory serves) sometime in May of 1999.

:Does this mean that there is no possibility to do what I want?
:If so, does that apply to the boxes which are about to come out, to 
:present ones, or both?  Obviously, I'm not interested in a server but a 
:workstation (at least as long as a server costs substantially more).  
:What machines would you recommend to do what I want to do?

  Only in the relatively extreme portions of the performance spectrum 
  does one see any truely significant differences between a traditional 
  server and a traditional workstation -- most servers, for instance, 
  now have to include at least basic graphics support as part of the
  baseline configuration requirements.  In the "commodity performance"
  portion of the performance spectrum I expect you will be considering, 
  the differences are primarily in terms of packaging and licensing and 
  configuration support.  Just make sure you have a graphics controller 
  adequate for your needs, and that is supported by all of the target 
  operating systems.

:My second question concerns Windows(NT).  Assuming we're all running the 
:same (latest) versions of the software in question, is WindowsNT on 
:Alpha fully compatible with Windows95 or whatever the current version is 
:on Intel hardware, as far as Microsoft Word documents are concerned?  
:For my purposes, I need 100% compatibility: my colleagues should not 
:know or care what hardware I'm running on, and I don't want to have to 
:fiddle with anything.

  Asking Microsoft Windows NT questions among OpenVMS folks?  You are 
  a brave soul. :-)   (You'll want to direct your compatibility questions 
  to the folks at Microsoft, in other words...)

:I guess another possibility would be Word for VMS or some sort of Intel
:hardware emulator (I've seen these running on IBM Unix boxes and even
:Word documents being produced there).  Do such beasts exist?  If so,
:would the 100% compatibility I need be possible?  If this is a viable 
:way to go, it seems that this is what I would prefer.

  Hardware and software emulators do/did exist, though with the "near-
  disposible" prices for computer systems these days, that approach 
  hardly seems worth the effort...  Two boxes and a video switchbox?

	--

  The AlphaStation series -- AlphaStation 200 and AlphaStation 255 --
  make nice platforms.  Later versions of these systems are half-flash, 
  meaning that you'll have to reload the console when switching between 
  Microsoft Windows NT and OpenVMS (from floppy, CD, or network) -- but 
  both support the operating systems of interest and share graphics 
  controllers such as the PowerStorm 3D30, and systems such as the 
  AlphaStation 255 4/300 move along quite nicely.  (Make *sure* you have 
  enough physical memory configured in the system, of course.)

  Most any recent PCI-based Alpha platform -- including the -au series
  Personal Workstation series -- will fill your needs.  The key to the
  configuration is usually ensuring the graphics and other I/O widgets
  are supported by the target operating systems, and remembering to
  have at least as many disks as operating systems.  (And don't believe
  Microsoft Windows NT when it refers to any disk operations it wants 
  to perform on disks it does not own as "harmless".)

:Replies to the newsgroups please.

  Gladly.

