| [Licensing and restrictions on use of staticly linked libraries on
| other systems]
| But I believe that copyright is a red-herring here.  Certainly it
| would be illegal to copy dynamic-link libraries or library archives
| verbatim.  But a program compiled with static linking is completely
| different.  The resulting binary program contains only parts of some
| library archives and they are no longer in archive format nor are the
| archives recoverable.

Nevertheless, they linked programs *do* contain copies of the copyright-
ed library material.  It's by no means necessary to copy an entire
copyrighted object - copying of anything more than a trivial portion can
infringe.
 
| Most importantly the binary program has significant new and original
| input that was not present in the libraries, and copyright law allows
| this.  While in many circumstance it is illegal to copy a photograph
| belonging to somebody else, it is perfectly OK to make and publish an
| artistic montage of a dozen copyrighted photographs, provided there is
| significant new input.

This is a novel reading of copyright law that is not supportable in the
US, or in most of the world.  I suppose it's possible that there is such
an exception in French law, though I'm skeptical - French law in general
is even more protective of the artist than the concensus of world
copyright law, recognizing various "moral rights" enjoyed (especially)
by visual artists.

The artistic montage would, in general, be treated like a collection of
short stories:  The individual photographs (and stories) would be
protected, *and* the combination - if it had significant new and
original input - would also be protected.  The rights involved are
complementary, not conflicting.  The only way an artistic montage would
be exempt would be if the individual elements were so reduced or
otherwise manipulated as to be essentially unrecognizable as what they
originally were.  This is certainly not the case for a program linked
with a static library.
 
| If your claim was true, then even shipping dynamically linked programs
| would be a violation of copyright since wrappers like crt0.o are
| linked in!

They probably would be if the provider chose to make them so!  (Of
course, many crt0.o's are so minimal that they would fall under various
exceptions, like being effectively the only way to "express" what they
are doing.)

None of this is really all that new.  Vendors of compilers - especially
3rd-party vendors, who couldn't make any money from additional hardware/
OS sales that grew out of the availability of products for their systems
- have often charged fees for the right to ship products that incor-
porate their RTL's.  After all, from the vendor's point of view, why is
a library that happens to be specified with a compiler any different
from any other library - say, a collection of efficient linear algebra
routines?  There are certainly companies that have *chosen* not to
charge for such redistribution rights - but I'd be surprised if you can
find any signficant company that didn't have you at least sign a
license, even if you weren't charged for it.

As vendors of complete systems have moved to dynamically linked
libraries, they've generally followed the path that DEC blazed with VMS:
The libraries come with the OS.  You pay only for the compiler; anyone
running the OS *already has* the libraries.  Third-party vendors, who
don't have their libraries shipped with the OS, have generally been
forced to provide equivalent access (i.e., libraries that can be
redistributed for free) wherever they had to compete with the system
vendors; but where they can get away with it, they charge for the
dynamic libraries, too.  The restrictions on redistribution of
statically linked programs probably remains, but is mainly of little
importance

What's new here is the desire to move the compiled code to a *different*
OS.  Now, if there were Linux dynamic libraries with the same entry
points as the Tru64 ones, there would be no ground for anyone to
complain - library entry point names and such are unlikely to be copy-
rightable or protectable in any other way.  But no such libraries exist.

| However this area is certainly a "grey area".  I get the impression
| that some people maintained the F.U.D. here when Alpha Linux clusters
| became fashionable.  Presumably some marketing idiot figured that by
| this kind of scare-tactics they could make a pretty penny by forcing a
| few thousands of copies of Digital Unix onto people buying Alpha
| workstations for clusters.  Instead they problaby succeeded in halving
| the market for said workstations.

I see little *grey* here about the underlying law. I have *no idea* what
the terms of Compaq's licenses are, but that could be resolved pretty
easily by checking the language - or, if you can't figure it out, asking
Compaq and, if you disagree with their interpretation, with a lawyer.

Before dismissing the claim that they have the *right* to prevent people
from running statically linked code on a non-Compaq OS - whether it's a
good idea for them to do it or not - consider:  If you think so little
of Compaq's library code, why not just write a Compaq-compatible dynamic
library for Linux?  Not a project you feel like undertaking now?  Why? 
It's not as if it's at all hard to find out what all the entry points
are and what they do.  Duplicating it, though, is a fair bit of work.
You don't want to do it - but you don't think Compaq is entitled to
protect the work *they've* done (even if that happens to be a bad
business decision)?
							-- Jerry



