Others have suggested something similar - why not drag the entire glibc
environment over to Digital UNIX, and recompile all its code using the
DEC C compiler and Alpha assembler.  With the right combination of
compiler and linker flags, you won't get any of DEC's run time code in
the resulting libraries.  Then, you could link your code against those
libraries either on DU or Linux and have a pure, non DEC program.  This
case might be simpler because you won't have any new code to write, but
you'll just have to fix any dependencies glibc has on gcc.  I understand
glibc is more or less compliant with ANSI C.

