It sounded like the original issues was a feature of the DEC Fortran
compiler that compiled code for a library routine that does four square
roots at a time very efficiently. As in a DEC supplied library routine
(which is presumeably under license) adds something like an instruction to
"do four square roots at a time" which the compiler knows how to use.

I don't see anyway around that if you're going to use the DEC compiler with
this feature turned on. Probably a better way to do this is to teach
another compiler (say egcs) to generate as good a code as is used in that
library routine. This should result in a more flexible approach, but is of
course a hell of a lot of work.

