You can look up the articles in the Carnegie-Mellon archives.  Its been 25
years since I've last looked at them, so I don't remember the details.  They
were published in peer-reviewed journals and considered good science.  The
context was reliability for extreme conditions such as the space program.
The issue I was specifically thinking of was the concept of dynamic voting
and extended dynamic voting.  I.e. if 3-way voting improves reliability,
then does 5-way or 7-way help things even more?  The answer was that it
depends on the reliability of the components.  A 7-way voter has more points
of failure than a 3-way voter.  A 3-way voter has more points of failure
than a single component.  Increasing the number of of points of failure can
decrease reliability.  The analysis was through and the reasoning is
straightforward.

My point was that there are many ways to design for reliability, especially
when cost is a part of the design goals.  Customers care more about results
than methods.  I might think that critizing your competitor's methods
instead of emphasizing your own strengths suggests you don't want to compare
results.


