Chris (icq@dial.pipex.com) wrote:
: I think you're missing the point - The reduction in mtbf is not because
: of the redundant components, but because of the extra hardware required
: to to interconnect them and switch out the failure.

This is basically not true. Most failure models give reduced or barely
increased reliability through redundancy *unless* you have repair.

First, you don't stand a chance until you know if a component is working or
not. Redundancy is often designed in to achieve "failfast" operation.
Failfast implies that it is known with very very high probability whether
whether a component is working correctly or has failed. (There is no
inbetween ground for a single component in most failure models I've seen.)
Two (of many) ways to do this are as follows:

- Take two components and join them with a comparator unit. If the outputs
compare the same, signal the unit operational and deliver the output.
Otherwise mark the unit failed. (One must use redundant comparators and
wires as well in general.)

- Take three components and compare their outputs. If one unit miscompares,
mark it as failed. Return the majority vote as the correct answer. If a
miscompare happens after one unit has failed, mark the entire unit as
failed.

The MTTF for the first situation is 1/2 the MTTF of a single component.
When it fails, we don't know which of the two components have failed. In
order to achieve a repairable system, we must pair the newly made failfast
component again. If we do this, we end up with an MTTF of 1.5 * .5 or .75
that of a "completely vanilla" component for four times the cost. If we had
failfast components to begin with, we could have gotten a factor of 1.5
improvement by duplicating them. (A factor of 1.5 improvement for twice the
cost is not particularly interesting usually.)

In the second case, we get a MTTF of 5/6 that of a single component.
(MTTF/3 for the first one to fail plus MTTF/2 for the second one to fail.)
But the system is repairable. You know which of the three components
miscompared and can replace it.

The above analyses ignore comparator failures and such on the assumption
that we've made that probability small.

So we have here situations with lower MTTF and ones with not much higher
MTTF for twice the cost. And such things are actually used in real designs.
Why? Because when combined with repair, we can deliver much much higher
reliability. (Many orders of magnitude better.) If the system is
repairable, there will be a small period of time (called the Mean Time To
Repair or MTTR) during which it is more vulnerable to failure. However if
it is repaired promptly, the system will be good as new again.

(I've got somewhere else to be so I'll spare you the MTTF with MTTR
calculations. If you're interested, see chapter 3 of
_Transaction_Processing:_Concepts_and_Techniques_ by Jim Gray and Andreas
Reuter.)

Note also that the above is assuming MTTF for hard failures. Transient
failures can be very effectively masked using redundancy techniques.

(As an aside, the reference given for this section of the above named book
is from _The_Theory_and_Practice_of_Reliable_System_Design_ by Siewiorek
and Swarz, 1982 . I believe Siewiorek was at Carnegie Mellon in 1982 and
that (or papers leading up to it) might be the reference Patrick was
mentioning.)

-Z-



