> 

Exactly. RAS stands for Reliability, Availability and Servicability

Reliability is obvious, components with higher MTBFS, good design 
reduced component counts.

Availability is obvious.

Servicability is easy repair of failed or still running systems.
Now some aspects of servicability are covered by things like 
good system access, modular designs, jumperless components etc
etc. But being able to replace failed components while 
the system is online is a big part of this.

In the case of the V-Class, you pay the penalty of additional 
complexity for redundant power supplies and cooling, with 
power distribution busses, variable speed fans and 
rotation sensors. Of course that means that if a PSU/FAN
fails the system does not.  

However you cannot repair that component once it has 
failed without halting the system because the PSU 
and FANS have not been packaged into hot pluggable 
units. You instead have to defer the repair until 
it is practical to shut the system down. Or you
configure it in a cluster.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



