> > > Lets try a little maths. Lets say we have a failure rate
> > > hypothetically of 1 disk per 100 per month. This means that
> > > with 100 non mirrored disks I will have lost data once a month.
> > >
> > > If I have 200 disks mirrored I will have lost 2 disks per month
> > > but instead of having a 100% chance of losing data I have
> > > a ?1% chance of lost data, even less than this If I replace
> > > my failed drives.
> > >
> >
> >   Yes but suppose that some of the faults are hard power supply
> > faults that take out the power supply causing all your disks to fail.
> > Or suppose you have 1 disk per 100 per month that fails, but out of
> > that 1 disk per ten years fails with dense black smoke emerging that
> > sets off the buildings sprinkler systems?
> >
>
> Suppose you put strings of disks on redundant or separate
> power supplies, this by the way is the norm, and mirror
> between strings of disks. This means that a PSU failure
> for a string of disks causes all the disks to fail but does
> not cause you to have lost data since the other leg of the
> mirror is still functioning.
>
>

  Of course, but what about the sprinkler system?



> >   Redundancy doesn't always work. Putting another booster on the
> > Challenger would of only made the explosion bigger.
> >
>
> True, but what we are talking about is reducing the balance
> of probablity that a system will fail completely. No
> Vendor will guarantee to provide a system that is 100%
> available because even with fully redundant systems
> running in lock step (Tandem, Stratus, Sun N1800) there
> is still a small chance of dual failures.

Absolutely, someone made the perfectly valid statement that
redundancy _can_  ( and often does) reduce reliability. Obviously
well designed redundancy that protects the _most_ frequent failure
modes will greatly improve reliability. But there is always some
penalty.

  For example take a backup generator, for a computer and the air conditioning
needed for a large installation. To implement this you need a switch that disconnects
the power from the mains so that your generator doesn't try to run the whole
city.

  If you have been having frequent power outages then the generator greatly
increases your reliability. But if you never have power outages, then the
chance that the switch fails sometime; during say the monthly generator test
or because someone thought it was the switch to reset the coffee pot circuit
is not that small.  Putting in the system can decrease the reliability.


 Reviewing the statements that started this:

> > ? There are many ways to design a system for high reliability.  It was shown
> > ? by studies as long ago as the early 1970's at Carnegie-Mellon that too much
> > ? redundancy could actually reduce a system's reliability by increasing the
> > ? total component count and thus the number of parts that might fail.  I'm not
> > ? saying that you couldn't increase the reliability of the V-class design.
> > ? You just about always can find ways to make a design more reliable.  But
> > ? those choices have costs, and even commerical customers are cost sensitive.
> > ? Sun of all companies is aware of cost vs volume tradeoffs in system design.
> > ?
> >
> > I am sorry but this is rubbish.
> > Redundant components do not cause systems to fail, of course you may
       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> > have a lower total mean time between component failures, because
> > you have more components. But if I lose a fan or PSU or an address
> > router or a clock, so what the component fails the system does not
> > because I have redundancy.
> >

  Yes they do! In well designed systems the number of times they save you
is greater than the number of times they kill you.

  My wife just had a lot of grief with a computer that wouldn't stay up. It runs
the library she works at.  Problem? The UPS was failing.





