Sander Vesik <sander@haldjas.folklore.ee> writes:

>In comp.arch Maynard Handley <handleym@ricochet.net> wrote:

>> Huh? Why is a common binary executable ipso facto a bad idea?

>Simple:
>	a) they are not talking about binary executable format (say
>	   elf that almost everybody supports anyways) but ABI
>	b) a common ABI is *BAD*. For it tends to mandate idiotic things
>	   like that file offsets be 32bit on 32bit systems like x86.
>	   And this does include Linux.

C'mon; you think the people why design such common ABIs are short-sighted;
and you think the OSS community will be excluded from defining it?

I believe that we should have had name mangling for C libraries years ago;
it would have made it easy to change fundamental types (the new routines
would have had a different name, the old routines would coexist)

An ABI should not be seen as "cast-in-stone", instead, it should be
developed with future extensibility in mind.

Casper
