"H.W. Stockman" wrote:
> 
> Peter Seebach wrote:
> 
> > I think they learned from the horrors of NT on the Alpha.  They want to make
> > sure that something does *well* on their architecture.
> 
> Do Unix/alpha programs really outperform the NT/alpha
> programs by a great deal?  A few years back, MS VC++
> didn't do all that well with alpha, because the MS wrapper
> couldn't hand the GEM core what it needed for highest
> optimization.  But I think that problem was largely
> fixed.  We did some Unix/NT benchmarks, and the NT-compiled
> codes were pretty close to the DU-compiled versions (10% slower). The
> principal difference seemed to arise from the fact that the
> NT VC++ compiler basically ignored register declarations.

I don't think it really has anything to do with the OS, but more
with the compiler.  We were seeing a 0-20% performance hit when
comparing our code on NT and Unix Alphas due to lack of certain
compiler optimizations in DVF 5.0d.  I haven't run any new 
benchmarks of our code recently, but from a "feel" perspective,
DVF 6.0 seems to generate code that is more on the level of the
DEC Unix Fortran 90 compiler.    

Jeff



