In article <7a1b9i$cd2$2@stronghold.dhp.com> mikesw@whiterose.net writes:
>
>Hi,
 
  Hello :-)

>   How can one do 900 MFLOPs when the machine can only do 600mhz;
>assuming no memory to processor speed prbolems and I can do 600MIPS?

  "Superscalar" processors are capable of performing multiple 
operations per clock cycle.  In the 21264's case, IIRC it can
issue and retire up to four instructions (two integer and two 
float) per clock cycle.  This gives you a theoretical top 
speed of 2400MIPS at 600MHz.  Since real-life code (1) can't 
always keep all four pipes busy, (2) does have to deal with 
the memory to processor speed problem, (3) sometimes stalls 
the pipeline (with mispredicted branches, etc) at big cost, 
and (4) sometimes has to use multi-cycle operations, the real
work done per clock cycle is usually a fraction of theoretical
maximum, but usually higher than 1MIPS per MHz.

  All processors I'm aware of which are being manufactured today
for the desktop or server markets are superscalar (or VLIW, which 
is a different way of doing the same sort of thing), with ranges 
from 2 to 8 (or 19, if you count N-way SIMD instructions to count 
as N instructions) theoretical MIPS per MHz.  Processors' actual 
work-per-unit-time is not at all obvious from examining their MIPS 
numbers, however, which is why people use the SPEC95 benchmark 
instead (which is closer -- *all* benchmarking techniques short of 
timing your target application on your system are wrong, but some 
are less wrong than others, and many people believe that SPEC95 
and TP-C are the least wrong of the benchmarks available).  

  -- TTK




