> "Thus, despite the fact that SPEC benchmarks are often discussed as just
> processor benchmarks, they actually emphasize three components of a system:
>
>      processor,
>      memory hierarchy, and
>      compiler.
>
> All of these components should be kept in mind when considering SPEC95 results"
>
>         So your point that they don't consider VM is incorrect.

In my experience working with SPEC benchmarks
(working on benchmarks that eventually got included
by SPEC), SPEC's consideration of VM for the CPU
benchmarks is principally to ensure that paging is
NOT happening.  I.e. things like "the benchmark should not
page on a 64MB machine" were the rule.  Nowadays it is
probably a 1GB machine.  I doubt that it is a >4GB machine.

I.e. I think that it is fair to say that the SPEC CPU benchmarks,
CIBT95 and CFP95, go out of their way to minimize OS and
VM interaction.   There may be some OS and VM interaction
at program startup and closedown times.

I think when they say "memory hierarchy" they mean registers,
L1 and L2 caches, and main memory.





