You are correct that I overlooked 10.30.  I forgot about it because that OS
is not in wide use, as it was soon superseded by 11.0.  But I still don't
see what is strange about requiring the latest OS which has full support for
Pthreads and 64-bit applications when releasing a new 16-way parallel
machine which often sells with much more than 4Gbytes of memory.  Reducing
the number of OS versions which we support cuts our support costs without
loss of functionality by the customer.  You are still pushing SUN sales FUD.

>The OS differences are only one part of the story though.
>
>The V-Series is being sold as a commercial server into 
>environments that need a high degree of reliability, but
>the V-Series's only RAS features apart from the normal ECC
>stuff is redundant power and redundant cooling. It has 
>no hot pluggable components to assist servicability for 
>example. In many respects its reliability facilities 
>are worse than commodity PC servers, Compaq for example 
>provide hot pluggable, Fans, PSU, and selected PCI 
>adaptors in the Proliants. 
>
>Now given the V-Series HPC design background this is not 
>all that suprising but as a commercial server product 
>it is remarkable. No other UNIX server vendor sells 
>a system so unsuited to one major requirment from 
>the commercial server market.
>
>Of course HP could suggest that the lack of these 
>capabilities was simply a design descision, but 
>then one has to query the wisdom of HP's descison.
>
>Since the decision was in fact made by Convex 
>in the light of the specific requirments of the 
>HPC market it is less suprising.
>

There are many ways to design a system for high reliability.  It was shown
by studies as long ago as the early 1970's at Carnegie-Mellon that too much
redundancy could actually reduce a system's reliability by increasing the
total component count and thus the number of parts that might fail.  I'm not
saying that you couldn't increase the reliability of the V-class design.
You just about always can find ways to make a design more reliable.  But
those choices have costs, and even commerical customers are cost sensitive.
Sun of all companies is aware of cost vs volume tradeoffs in system design.

The current design focused on minimizing failures in each component rather
than trying to hotswap all parts.  I think they succeeded.  The V-class has
an excellent track record, and HP has put specific customer requirements in
contracts with appropriate clauses for non-performance.  The commercial
server market has a wide variability of reliability requirements, ranging
all the way up to full redundant system coverage with immediate fall-over
operation.  HP supports these options.  The V-class continues to get large
numbers of orders and has many referencable customers.  It sounds to me like
the previous poster has been reading too much of Sun's internal sales
literature.

I'm trimming the I/O discussion because I don't see any point to the
objections.  Basically, a design decision was made which excluded other
choices.  If we had made a different choice, Sun sales might well be saying
we should have chosen PCI to lower our customer costs.  My response is to
tell a customer to run their real application benchmarks on these systems
and then see what performance is available.  Over my 11 years in the
supercomputer/high performance computing business, I've seen too many I/O
products by too many vendors marketed using peak specs when the actual
application performance is dramatically less than the peak.

I'm trimming the small vs large customer issue because I'm in engineering,
not marketing.  There are all sorts of ways to run a business.  Different
models work best for different folks.  When you buy a car, do you go to
General Motors or Ford?  Or do you go to your local dealer.  Even buyers of
fleets of cars go to their local dealers.  I'll bet I get better treatment
from my local dealer than I would if I tried to call the Ford home office.

- Patrick McGehearty
