In comp.arch TTK Ciar <ttk@cygnus.com> wrote:
> In article <36C20667.B7537A@cs.wisc.edu> Andy Glew <glew@cs.wisc.edu> writes:
>>
>>E.g. Alpha, if it succeeds in the mass-market, risks cannibalizing
>>its high-end systems - why buy VMS or OSF if LINUX does the job?

>   I don't think there's too much danger here.  Most VMS customers
> need VMS's clustering and fault tolerance capabilities, and Linux
> can't give them what they need.

Probably true.  Or they just have legacy software for VMS.

> Similarly, I don't know if even
> the new 2.2.x kernel scales as well as OSF on SMP systems.

But here lies the potential problem for Compaq.  I'm certain most
Alpha systems have 4 or fewer processors, and Linux should run OK
on them.  If Tru65 Unix (hey, you're two revisions behind on the
renaming of OSF :-) is only used by those with monster systems, the
volume won't justify the development budget.

One partial solution would be for workstation companies to ship
a Linux distribution, but without the Linux kernel itself,
replacing it with their own kernel.  That cuts down on the
work needed for user space, and lets them concentrate on the
bits they are good at, like high performance filesystems,
clustering, very high levels of SMP, etc.

Certainly, the shell and tools on an as-shipped Solaris
system are a disaster, and most competent sysadms start by
installing half a Linux distro on them to make them useful.
Why not rationalise by shipping them that way and save money
at the same time?

> I would
> imagine that providing Linux on the systems which Linux can handle
> (ie, 4-way-or-less Alpha, low fault-tolerance requirements) would 
> be *more* profitable to Compaq since it would lower the price of 
> the system and allow them to sell more units.

It lowers the price of the system by taking money from the OS
division, but it doesn't lower the costs from the OS division,
since producing half as many copies of the software doesn't make
it much easier to write.

