This is incorrect, HP-UX 10.30 supports Pthreads and it 
pre-dates HP-UX 11. 64 bit support HP-UX 11's other major 
feature is desirable but not necessary on the V-Series.
Sun, Sequent, DG and a number of other companies have all 
had >4 GB memory machines with 32 bit OS's. 

HP-UX 10.20 would have been totally impractical since 
it isn't threaded and a 16 CPU SMP machine without a 
threaded OS is probably not that usefull.

What was stranger was the decision to initiall only release 
HP-UX 11 on the V-Series.

The OS differences are only one part of the story though.

The V-Series is being sold as a commercial server into 
environments that need a high degree of reliability, but
the V-Series's only RAS features apart from the normal ECC
stuff is redundant power and redundant cooling. It has 
no hot pluggable components to assist servicability for 
example. In many respects its reliability facilities 
are worse than commodity PC servers, Compaq for example 
provide hot pluggable, Fans, PSU, and selected PCI 
adaptors in the Proliants. 

Now given the V-Series HPC design background this is not 
all that suprising but as a commercial server product 
it is remarkable. No other UNIX server vendor sells 
a system so unsuited to one major requirment from 
the commercial server market.

Of course HP could suggest that the lack of these 
capabilities was simply a design descision, but 
then one has to query the wisdom of HP's descison.

Since the decision was in fact made by Convex 
in the light of the specific requirments of the 
HPC market it is less suprising.

