DEC Unix comes with the DEC Unix C/C++, and, I think, Fortran
compilers.  Even if these eventually become available for Linux they
will raise the cost of the Linux system to near the cost of a DEC Unix
license.

The problem I see for/with DEC Unix is that it isn't readily
available, it isn't talked about much, and its userbase is pitifully
high-end.

My Uni went with a mixed IBM/SGI setup that doesn't meet their needs
as well as a DEC setup would have, because DEC ran into those nice
problems with MIPS and DEC wasn't active enough in countering FUD.

My work uses Sun boxen and Solaris because they are almost as common
in educational circles as AIX boxen, and are fairly common in the real
world as well.  n rarely has the best performance, but they are less
well-known for orphanning boxen/hardware (even though there isn't a
cg12 driver in Solaris since 2.4 ^_^ )

DEC, it seems to me, really should evangelize a little bit more for
their operating systems.

Give out free demo OpenVMS Alpha kits, with six-month licenses.

Set up a hobbyist/educational license for DEC Unix, so they can gather
the support of the people who make, and will make, purchasing
decisions for sites that are now going with Sun/HP/IBM/WinNT not
because of any technical merit, but because that is what they know.

Sure, this will cut into their profits a tiny bit, however I'm sure
that there are at least a few people who, like me, don't have a DEC
Unix license for their Multia/AS200/DEC 3000 because they can't
justify spending two to twelve times their hardware cost for a DEC
Unix license, and about their hardware cost for a media kit . . .

The OS is already paid for anyway; Corp. upgrades seem to be enough to
cover their development costs already, so any sales to the tiny people
are profit.

Purely IMHO,
