Leon Muratov wrote:

> And speaking about recompiling, can anybody explain me why g77 sucks so badly
> on "fringe architectures". Actually, I only heard about Alpha (I am new to
> it) but I had an experience with HP-PA (it times not %).
                                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
I'm sorry, I can't parse this sentence :-)

I know too little about the HPPA architecture (execpt that it uses a
particular brain-dead assembler, which dark corners g77 was hitting
regularly).

On the Alpha, the reasons for slow executables (wrt Digital's
compiler(s)):

1. No 4-sqrts-at-a-time library routine.  This will be moot with
   the 21264, which has a hardware sqrt instruction.
2. Complex doesn't work the way it was designed to, due to bugs in the
   backend of the compiler dealing with pairs of 32-bit floats on
   64-bit architectures.
3. Glibc math routines are not (yet) up to speed with Digital's.
   There are alternatives (see http://www.alphalinux.org).
4. Compiling LAPACK with g77 (or Digitals compiler) won't give you
   much performance.  If you want to do linear algebra at close to
   900 MFLOPs on a 600 Mhz 21164, you have to use specially crafted
   assembler (again, see http://www.alphalinux.org)

That's it, off the top of my head.

