In article <79j8j5$goo$1@sh1.ro.com>, gheston@ro.com (Gary Heston) writes:
:  I picked up a VAX 6210 recently, which had with it one rack containing
:two RA-60-00 drives, and an Emulex rack containing two SM80 controllers,
..
:  Presumably, the RA-60 specs are on the CompECital (or whatever) site
:somewhere. 

  The RA60 specifications were posted elsewhere.

  I am not familiar with the Emulex widgets.

  This VAX 6000 model 210 system likely has a KDB50 BI-to-SDI disk 
  controller or possibly a KDM70 XMI-to-SDI disk controller.  The
  controller will operate with the RA60, RA7x, RA8x, and RA9x
  series disk drives -- if you have a choice, you will want the 
  higher numbers among the RA7x or RA9x series drives (eg: RA74,
  RA92) for reasons of capacity, speed, size, and relative age...

  The SDI drives are generally old and large and consume vast 
  quantities of electrical power particularly when compared to 
  current (no pun intended) SCSI widgets.  Though the VAX 6000 
  series system isn't particularly economical, given the level 
  of performance (2.8 VUPS) compared to more recent systems.

  There are in-cabinet upgrades to more recent VAX 6000 series
  systems, though (IIRC) an upgrade to the VAX 6000 model 500 
  and VAX 6000 model 600 series CPUs will require new memory and 
  a new backplane.  IIRC, upgrades as far as the model 400 (7 VUPS
  per CPU) will work in your current cabinet.

:  I also got a pair of VAXstation 3100s with monitors, and a SGI
:Personal Iris with a 19" or 21" Sony Trinitron monitor. 

  The slowest VAXstation 3100 series systems are equivalent to the
  VAX 6000 model 210 series in performance, and most are faster
  (2.8 VUPs to 7.6 VUPS).  The VAXstation systems will also hook 
  more easily to SCSI widgets.  (Beware the 1.073 GB limit detailed 
  in the OpenVMS FAQ and in the http://www.openvms.digital.com/wizard/ 
  Ask The Wizard area.)

  Given a choice, I'd look for a VAX 4000 model 10x box, or an
  upper-end VAX 4000 Q-bus box, or any of the various VAXstation 
  4000 boxes.  Unless you currently use electric heat and can get 
