In article <1999Feb7.152825.1@eisner>, kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
:In article <36BDCB98.5CA54CD1@java-factory.com>, MR <mr@java-factory.com> writes:
:
:> I need to find cheap DEC HW for a study project. I'd be grateful for
:> some info on which ALPHA based system is the oldest / cheapest that will
:> run current VMS 7.2.
:> Must have (or be expandable to): 
:> * SCSI controller / disks / CD
:> * 1600 x 1200 x 64k graphics
:> * 512 M memory
:
:The oldest/cheapest machine that is still tolerable would likely be
:the DEC 3000-300 (with an LX after it sometimes).  It is slow (by
:Alpha standards) but of a more standard design than the DEC 150/PC
:(aka DEC 2000-200).

  The DECpc 150 AXP was also known as the DEC 2000 model 300 or
  as the (bigger box, more internal expansion) DEC 2000 model 500.

  The expandability of this system is extremely limited, and you
  also get to deal with EISA bus and associated widgets, and the 
  cabling limitations of the EISA SCSI controller, and (this is
  the kicker) the Compaq Qvision graphics controller -- I don't 
  think that particular widget will come anywhere near supporting 
  your required 1600 x 1200 x 64k graphics, and there were very
  few (one?) EISA supported graphics controllers under OpenVMS.

:                    The one thing I am not sure about is the memory
:capacity, and of course chips have gotten denser since it was made.

  Personally, I'd go for an AlphaStation 200 or AlphaStation 255
  series as a minimum -- these run OpenVMS quite nicely, (based on
  comments posted here by folks that have acquired them) are quite 
  cheap to buy, support PCI widgets rather than the TURBOchannel 
  I/O on the DEC 3000 series, usually have integrated SCSI, and 
  can also bootstrap Microsoft Windows NT if you are so inclined 
  (and have a second disk)...

  The AlphaStation 255 4/233 and 4/300 support up to 512 MB, the 
  bottom end (and really slow) AlphaStation 200 4/100 support up 
  to 192 MB.

  The PowerStorm 4D20 will likely be your choice for 1600 x 1200
  graphics under OpenVMS -- which precludes the use of the DECpc 
  150 AXP (DEC 2000) and the TURBOchannel (DEC 3000) boxes...

:I believe any Alpha that will run VMS is supported by V7.2.

  Correct -- support requirements have not changed, though the
  minimum physical memory required does tend to increase...

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
