Rhonda Ford wrote:
> 
> Regarding Digital (DEC) VT  Terminals (which was bought by Boundless
> Technologies)? I am working with our local phone company and they are
> trying to hook one to a Northern DMS 10 Switch. First they ordered a
> VT510 and it worked for a few days and then it started screwing up. We
> talked to various tech supports and phone companies in the area and
> could not figure it out. Finally Boundless suggested that we exchange
> for a VT520 which is the same terminal only 2 more Mb of memory. So we
> did. The VT520 ran for about a week and now it is screwing up.
> 
> They are communicating to/from the switch with the terminal and the
> terminal is sending all output to a Okidata Dot Matrix Parallel Printer
> attached to the terminal. The problem is that it intermittently will do
> one of the following after entering a command:
> 1) It will pause while producing the output on the screen (& printer),
> skip a few lines and then continue.
> 2) It will stop while producing the output on the screen (& printer),
> skip the whole command.
> 3) It will pause while producing the output on the screen (& printer),
> throw a bunch of garbage on the screen.
> 

The source of the problem is the Printer ... although it is reflected in
the terminal.

The printer buffer is being filled up and trying to exert some kind of
flow control on the terminal, which in turn will try to exert flow
control on the switch, which the switch manages to ignore.

Determine the flow control settings for the terminal, the switch and the
printer, and make sure all 3 are set in a compatible way.

For example, if the switch uses CTS/RTS, make sure that the terminal
does the same to the comm port connected to the switch.  If the switch
uses XON/XOFF again, make sure the terminal is set that way.

Same for the printer ... make sure the terminal is set to the same
protocol.

If the switch and or printer use hardware flow control, CTS/RTS, make
sure the connectors in use support the flow control selected.  It may be
that MMJ connectors (Like a modified telephone plug) won't.

Make sure the terminal is not set up to pass XOFF/XON transparently to
the host.

Hope this helps ...

Stuart



