I hate like hell to agree with the Reverend, but it still looks like it from
here simply because the people with clues don't generally make the decisions.

Not just purchase decisions. There's also things like "if we charge 5 times
as much for the UNIX version of our product than the NT version, even though
our development and support costs are lower, don't you think that will make
it all the more likely that Microsoft will take over and eat us?"

I mean, really. Microsoft's tendency to buy out and dismantle their vendors
is too bloody common.

(of course this hasn't anything to do with the point the Rev is trying to make)

>>         Rule To Live By:  If you're doing everything right but the machine
>> still isn't behaving, reboot.  Problem solved almost every time
>> unless it's hardware.  Time wasted going down blind alleys - 0
>> seconds.

>Nope. And Nope.

Now here the Rev Don Clueless loses.

If you simply reboot every time you have a problem, you haven't fixed the
underlying problem. You lost what you were working on and you have to wait
several minutes to get back to a state where you can continue working. And
inevitably it's going to happen again.

If you find out what's wrong, it may take some time, but you stop having to
reboot.

Now it could well be that Microsoft operating sustems are so broken that this
solution is not practical, but it's rare that a UNIX box doesn't yeild its
secrets when questioned. So, if you don't want to find yourself stalling
for time in the middle of a presentation, you know what to do...

Then again, we're in the realtime control business. An unexpected reboot
can kill people, so maybe we take things a bit more seriously.

