Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec

> >It's kind of strange, I think, that Intel seems to be embracing the
> >Open Source community, while the fringe architectures are more
> >reluctant.  After all, empowering users to recompile mainstream
> >software ought to be an advantage for the little guy, and undermining
> >the market domination advantage of the big player?

Possibly because Intel sells only hardware (and a little bit of software).
And Intel's end-users have typically been the mass market.

Whereas the fringe architectures (SGI/MIPS, Alpha, SUN, HP-PA)
typically have sold whole systems, to MIS or IT departments or the
like.  I.e. corporate customers who have been somewhat slower
to accept Open Source software.

E.g. Alpha, if it succeeds in the mass-market, risks cannibalizing
its high-end systems - why buy VMS or OSF if LINUX does the job?
Even if a non-Open Source commodity OS like NT succeeds,
the risk is of cannibalizing the high margin products.
Over some period of time, the extra profits earned by lower margins
on more mass market boxes have to outweigh the profits lost
by high margin boxes not being sold.






