Intel's hegemony wrt EV6/EV67 and related chipsets has been in clear evidence
lately. Their response to process and packaging issues has been glacial at
best. Yields above 583mhz are reported to be virtually non-existant (like
nobody saw that one coming, eh?) Yet they are pressing to raise the wafer
pricing to Compaq (which is stuck with buying full wafers regardless of
yield).

"Volume" means little if the functional yields suck. Intel may continue to
crank out wafers to make their end of the settlement, but lord knows where
those wafers will end up (my suggestion: use 'em for mobiles, or perhaps
"silicon skeet pigeons")...

On the other hand...API (Samsung) has been yielding over 50% at 600mhz or
better, and are pushing prices down to encourage wider adoption of EV.

The last thing in the world Intel wants is to help Alpha kick Xeon (now) and
Merced/McKinley's (later) collective ass. The first thing API wants is to do
the kicking.

Who would you put your money on to be there during the end-game?

One thing's for certain: the only person who actually made out in this whole
mess was Palmer...

/daytripper ("Alpha Instead" has a nice ring to it ;^)



