In article <79i3bn$bpq$1@server05.icaen.uiowa.edu>,
Doug Siebert <douglas-siebert@uiowa.edu> wrote:
>
>The moron (Rick Belluzo) that is
>currently at the helm of SGI was responsible for HP talking up NT and
>allowing people from Sun to talk about how HP was abandoning Unix (and
>did the same at SGI)  

I think that there has been some misinterpretation here.

Let's start by making it very clear that SGI has never announced
any intention to "abandon UNIX".  What we did say is that since
our customers kept on demanding NT for desktop systems (and,
increasingly, for small servers), we decided to build machines
that will run NT.  I can guarantee that this was driven overwhelmingly
by customer demand, and not at all by any bizarre management vision
of abandoning UNIX.   


>What is surprising to me is that SGI corrected itself, without giving
>Belluzo the boot.  So either he has reformed, saner heads in SGI's
>boad prevailed, or he quit getting payoffs from Redmond :)  

I think it is quite inaccurate to say that "SGI corrected itself".

We never announced any intention to drop UNIX systems -- we simply
did not discuss the UNIX on IA32 issue (until last week).  It has
always been clear that we were strongly supporting our UNIX offering
on our MIPS-based and (future) IA64-based servers, and that we were
continuing upgrades of the MIPS-based (UNIX) desktop systems for at
least the next couple of years.  All we have done is clarify that 
we will be promoting Linux on IA32 as the Unix-like offering, rather
than IRIX.


>Once SGI
>announced their NT strategy, didn't say anything to stop the worries
>and FUD about their abandoning Irix and MIPS (at least below the O2K
>line, which is more of a Cray product anyway) 
 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
What gave you this idea?   The Origin2000 was designed and
implemented entirely pre-merger.  The continuing development of the
Origin2000 and it successors are projects of the unified
engineering staff, but it should be obvious that the Origin2000
could not have been, since early customer shipments were made less
than three months after the merger finalized.



>and laid off the CPU designers and jettisoned MIPS, I figured there
>was no stopping him and really had written off SGI as being a future
>player in Unix.

Again, there is a major misinterpretation here.  Only a portion of
MIPS was spun off -- the embedded systems business (not a UNIX
stronghold!).  Only some of the processor design people were laid
off (though a number of people quit when H1 and H2 were cancelled)
-- most were transferred into server engineering to continue the
R12K/R14K development, or to work on system ASICs instead of
microprocessors.
-- 
