Allow me to clarify. The Digital Semiconductor arm of DEC wanted to go
into the fabrication business. That is why they built the billion-dollar
white elephant known as Fab-6. In order to justify the cost of the fab,
the proponents needed to have something to actually produce. Had DEC
elected to contract the manufacture of Alpha chips to someone else,
there would have been damn little left to produce in the Hudson plant.

Which of course was the case even with the Alpha franchise. Fab-6 never
ran at more than 30 percent capacity, despite fabbing StrongARM CPUs and
graphics chips in addition to Alpha.

I believe the DEC board of directors was snookered into the Fab-6
decision. Some of the circa-1990 internal documents on projected Alpha
volumes were, shall we say, outrageously optimistic.




