Chris,

First, I ordered a replacement lock & key from Compaq.  It set me back
all of $5.00.  When the lock arrived (took about 2 days from date of
order, but I'm about 30 mi. from the warehouse), I saw that it was the
typical circular key found in most DEC boxes I've ever encountered.
The part number for the lock/key matches the part number in the
maintenance manual for the beast.

So how did a flat key lock find its way into the box?  This remains a
mystery to me.

Then, I took your suggestion and took a 1/8" drill to the lock.
Drilled out enough to be able to jimmy the lock open with a
flat-bladed screwdriver.  Fairly quick and painless operation.
Painless for me anyway, but I was like a painless dentist.

Last step is to install the new lock, something I've done before.

Thanks for your suggestion.  Anybody want to buy a nice Pentium II
server with new lock?  ... Ben Myers

