Reece R. Pollack wrote in message <36d5c82f.1462717726@news4.his.com>...
>On Wed, 3 Feb 1999 11:11:50 -0500, "Fred Weng" <fweng@raptor.com>
>wrote:
>
>>Hi, all.  I bought a Alpha station 200 4/233 from an aution site without
>>manual.  When I boot it up, it has the VMS os.  The firmware is built in
>>with VMS.   I have no clue to set it up.  Could anybody help me or any
>>hints?  When I do "show config".  It gave me these info.
>>--------------------------------------------
>>SRM Console:    V6.2-2
>>Show_arc:            Can't find Flash Rom containing ARC console
>>PALcode:            VMS PALcode V5.56-2.  OSF PALcodex1.46-2
>>Serial Rom:        V4.6
>>DiagRom:            V.1.7
>>
>>Processor:        DECchip 21064A-6 233Mhz 512kb cache
>>
>>and some other i/o stuff.
>
>Digital supplied two different console subsystems (think BIOS) for
>these systems: SRM and ARC. SRM is used to boot OpenVMS and Digital
>Unix, while ARC is used to boot Win/NT. This specific machine has one
>set of console flash ROMs (there are sockets for another set) and has
>the SRM console loaded. What you're seeing is the SRM console, not
>OpenVMS.
>
>DECUS (Digital Equipment Corporation Users Society) has arranged for
>the availability of free OpenVMS licenses for non-commercial use.
>You'll have to hunt around for the correct website for more
>information if you want to run OpenVMS. I don't know much about the
>availability of Digital Unix for non-commercial use.
>
>This system can also run Win/NT, if you lust after the MicroSloth
>environment. You'd have to switch to the ARC console, but that's a
>five minute job. The console update software is available from the
>Digital website.
>
>Yet another option is to run Linux, which will boot under either the
>SRM or ARC consoles, and I believe the Linux loader MILO can also be
>loaded as a console.
>
>--
>Reece R. Pollack
>Senior Software Engineer
>Attachmate Specialty Products Group





