In article <79l1j1$ct9$1@murrow.corp.sgi.com>,
  mccalpin@engr.sgi.com wrote:
> We certainly hope to educate our customers -- but we also have to
> meet their needs, instead of our idealizations of what they need.

Well, often the "customer" isn't a single mind. At my last job, I heard about
a project another division had been working on. It was a printer server for a
high resolution, high speed color printer, and that server was a PC running
under SCO Unix. It was hidden in a small part of the printer, and connected
to the outer world via Ethernet. Management of the server was done by www,
and printer connection via lpr and SMB protocol - no monitor and no keyboard
was ever attached to this computer. It worked. Now the management of the
customer wanted it be a Windows NT box.

My coworker, who was involved in the first project asked the guy from the
customer why on earth they want it on NT, too. And he just said: "I'm only a
small guy, the boss wants so." They really spend quite a lot of money just to
be buzzword compliant, and I don't know if it was successful at all (there
were doubts about the performance of NTFS, because it already was close to
the file system throughput limit on SCO). How can you educate pointy haired
bosses? You have no chance to talk to them.

The last EDA forum had something similar: there was a workshop about EDA on
NT or Linux. The tool builders had lots of requests for NT, and no requests
for Linux. The engineers in the room wanted Linux (I suppose Linux/Alpha
preferred), and didn't want NT. Why was there a communication problem? Well,
EDA is so expensive that no engineer is allowed to decide. The pointy haired
boss two levels up doesn't know what the engineers want or need (like
scripting, stability, and performance), but has heard about NT.

Bernd Paysan
