The comment about Belluzo emphasizing NT and minimizing UNIX marketing is
something I think is correct.  After his departure, there were rumors that
he would have liked to totally deemphasis the Unix business and go full
steam for NT and the PC product line.  Of course, those rumors are
unconfirmed and may be urban myths/sour grapes.  They had a certain
plausibility because Belluzo had been in charge of the PCs and printers
division before the HPUX workstation products were added to his domain.
Failures by middle management to get approval for new spending on
workstation products might be blamed on "those PC guys who hate Unix",
without regard to the truth or merits of the specific situation.

But I have several points to take issue with in the rest of the posting.
For example, "seemed to stop spending" - well, advertising dollars for Unix
did drop under Belluzo, but headcount in the HPUX R&D group did not.  We
worker bees continued to work in the backrooms on new features and
functionality.

>You could say the same about Sun's aquisition of the E10000 
>from Cray but then Sun had a perfectly good competitor to 
>the V Series/8400/NUMA-Q 2000 in the E3X00-E6500 moreover 
>the E10K uses big chunks of Sun technology, the cross bar
>excepted. It was also designed as a commercial server 
>with more than just redundant power supplies as RAS
>facilities.
>
>The V-Series and its Convex/HPC alter ego also have much 
>less in common with the rest of the HP product range than 
>the E10000 does to the rest of the Sun range.
>
>The Convex boxes orgionally ran a completely different 
>version of HP-UX, the V-Series now require HP-UX 11 not 
>required for any other large HP server.
>
>The V-Series use PCI unlike any other large HP server.
>Before that Convex actually used SBus rather than HP-PB. 
>
>This all smelt of a quick fix.

This all sounds like more Sun FUD.

The original Convex boxes with PA-RISC processors were designed/built before
Convex was acquired by HP, so the OS team were forced to lag the HP OS
plans.  They also got sucked into the OSF microkernel hype which caused a
lot of grief all around.  Ancient history as far as the V-class is
concerned.  Starting with the V-class, the OS teams are fully integrated and
the V-class runs the mainstream OS release, HP-UX 11.  Older products
continue to run older OSes, but since the HP-UX 11 OS series was the first
to provide full pthreads support, it seems retrograde to offer an older OS
without pthreads for a highly parallel server.  Since the V-Series is the
largest and newest HP server, it should not be surprising that it expects to
use the newest OS.

On the I/O issue, I'm not up on all the internal performance details, but
why would we want to offer older, slower I/O busses on a new,
high-performance product?  Lots more new products are being developed for
PCI bus than any of the other choices, so our customers will have more
choices at better prices with PCI than with older busses.

>
>HP also re-organised their account coverage putting their 
>smaller customers through their distribution channel, with 
>their direct sales force concentrating on a few very large 
>customers, this had the effect of distancing them from 
>a lot of their UNIX customers and developers. 
>
Hmmm, I was at Interworks98 (annual HP users conference) last year with an
attendance of several thousand people.  There were lots of smaller customers
there as well as some bigger ones.  There also were lots of HP people
listening to the customer input at all levels of management and engineering
from the HPUX R&D groups.  [shameless plug: Interworks 99 is coming soon:
March 6-11 see http://www.interex.org/conference/IWorks99/ for details.
You might even find me at the conference.]

>This all lead to a perfectly reasonably perception bolstered 
>by analyst reports that HP was stepping back from UNIX. 
