Public statements by HP executives have indicated that a factor in HP's
renewed advertising for HPUX is due to the FUD Sun had been spreading about
HP's commitment (or lack of it) to HPUX.  Maybe we should thank Sun for
reminding us to tell our customers that HP still cares about Unix. :-)

Going back to the origins of this thread, the decision to emphasize HPUX had
nothing to do with any Merced schedule changes, beyond what has been
publicly disclosed.  Sales and Marketing are always focused on making quota
for the current quarter and current year, not on future products that can't
be shipped today.  Since HP couldn't sell Merced products in 1998, HP
advertised what we had to sell in 1998.  As far as having future PA-RISC
product plans in addition to IA-64 product plans, HP is big enough to pursue
multiple development paths for processors and systems.  Then they back
whichever ones work the best for the customer.  There are always some
customers who want the latest stuff and other customers who want to stay
with things that have been tested by time.  A smart company supports both
types of customers.  In the midst of the early Merced hype, we had neglected
to convey that message to our customers.  That oversight has been corrected.

