Just as an aside... the reason for the limitations on this system are
due to the system design, and limits in VMS.  The system was designed
with no PCI (EISA-only) which limits cards to ISA or EISA.  In addition,
the frame buffer memory for the EISA card needs to be located (if memory
serves me) the low 16MB.  In addition, "memory" space and "ISA memory
space" overlap in regards to DMA, so a window needs to be carved out for
the frame buffer - VMS does not have the concept of "types" of memory
where DMA is possible, and where it is not.

So, the 1MB frame buffer is placed at PA 1MB, since the console is
loaded there, and no DMA ever occurs to that range.

I did about 95% of the work to support the Mach-32 on the axp150 - but
it went EOL, and the Mach-64 needed a 8mb aperture on a 8mb boundry, so
it would need to be loaded at PA 8mb, for 8mb.  And within that 8mb
window, and real memory in that range could not be the target of a
DMA... so I punted the whole idea of worrying about some other adapter.

If the console does not come up, and you get the out-of-range error --
my guess is that the console BIOS emulation is having a hard time with
the video BIOS.  We noticed this a lot on the EISA platforms - oddball
things in the video BIOS would prevent the card from working at all.

_Fred



