andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com wrote:
> 
> "Terry C. Shannon" wrote:

> > Well, DEC and MIPS **did** partner for a while (DEC owned a small chunk
> > of MIPS, I believe) but DEC got burned one time too many.
> >
> > The MIPS silicon always was a day late and a dollar short.
> >
> 
> The origional DECstations based on the MIPS processor
> were very competative from a performance standpoint. They
> had better floating point performance than the Sun SPARCstation 1
> but they were packaged badly if my memory is correct.

The systems announced on Jan 10 1989 were in fact the fastest
workstations available at the time. Dunno if the packaging was
particularly bad, but apps availability was a Big Issue. DEC claimed 45
or 52 apps on Day One, but could only demo a dozen or so.

> 
> Later MIPS boxes from Digital such as the MP machines were
> less competitive, though much of the blame for this has
> to be laid at the door of ULTRIX and the System design.
> 
> One of my customers was the happy recipent of a second
> MIPS CPU for their DEC MP server, they were less happy
> to discover that the thing went slower with 2 CPUs in it
> than it did with one.

Sounds like the DECsystem 5800, a VAX 6000 SMP box jury-rigged with MIPS
CPUs. You are entirely correct, adding CPUs (especially the third, and
you didn't even think about CPU Number Four) did tend to slow the thing
down. The 100MB/sec XMI-bus backplane was one of the culprits here.

> 
> I know nothing about the R6000 project except that it
> wasn't a sucess but I though that Digital were doing Alpha
> before then anyway.

The R6000 project (as embodied in the DECsystem 5900, I believe) was
underway before the decision was made to go to Alpha. In fact, for a
time DEC was thinking about extending and enhancing the R-whatever
architecture in lieu of doing Alpha (then known as EVAX).

As an aside, I believe that Sun's success with the initial Sparc-based
products was one of the reasons DEC elected to scuttle the PRISM
architecture and jump to MIPS, at least as an interim solution.

Terry Shannon




