andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com (andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com) wrote:
: Well they were never very keen on Alpha from the start. One MPR 
: article I read chastised Digital for dropping MIPS and 
: going with their own design. The article suggested that 
: Digital were pointlessly adding another RISC processor to 
: the market for the sake of something akin to penis envy.

This is a little different than the bias subject. I still believe that had
DEC commited their engineering resources to the MIPS architecture, and had
MIPS cooperated in doing some of the things DEC had to have, the
marketplace would be somewhat different today. If you believe in the
proposition of standards and open systems, etc. then DEC would have done
very well. On the other hand, many companies have demonstreated that they'd
rather not face the competition that an open standard brings. (E.g. Apple
with clone vendors.) DEC didn't go that route, but its quite legitimate to
point out that if they had, their main architecture might be the dominant
one today.

Furthermore, it was obvious that once DEC did Alpha, any notion that either
MIPS or Alpha was going to make a dent in Intel's desktop marketshare was
history.

: Of course since then MIPS has languished from a performance 
: standpoint while gaining considerable market share in the 
: Game/Settop/Embedded market while Digital produce arguably 
: if you are HP the fastest CPU on the planet while having the 
: smallest market penetration.

Has relatively little to do with the MIPS architecture vs. the Alpha
architecture in my opinion. Lots to do with SGI's priorities.

: You could arge as MRP did that MIPS and Digital would have 
: done better in partnership rather than competing but then hindsight 
: is a 20:20 science.

MPR (and I and many others) argued it at the time it happened. That's not
hindsight. To many of us, it felt like stating the obvious. And the fallout
has been significant. DEC never became a notable player in the NT market.
Their enterprise systems revenue got better but was not great compared to
many competitors. And now the company itself doesn't exist in name
anymore. So if someone was planning on having Alpha save the company, then
they blew it.

On the other hand...

The claim was that Alpha was the architecture for the next 25 years. At the
moment, it looks like there's a chance someone will still be building Alpha
boxes in 2015. That perception(*) will evaporate overnight if the IA64
miracle occurs. But maybe we're seeing the unfolding of a brilliant
longterm architectural insight that you had to be brutally simple and
64-bit and optimized for high-clockspeed in 1990 in order to be king of the
hill in 2005. Even if that decision meant suffering for 15 years.

(I'd sort of like that to be the case because it makes computer
architecture very important. But somehow I can't quite believe it.)

Oh yeah, we mustn't forget those patents which came in really handy at a
crucial time...

-Z-

(*) With the caveat that conventional wisdom is far more fickle than
accurate. Two years ago, many were certain Alpha would be dead and gone by
now. Crushed under the volume of IA-64 chips shipped :-)



