Terry C. Shannon (shannon@world.std.com) wrote:
: Well, DEC and MIPS **did** partner for a while (DEC owned a small chunk
: of MIPS, I believe) but DEC got burned one time too many.

DEC was MIPS' major licensee. I think the relationship was announced in
late '88 or early '89 and was going strong until late '90 to mid '91.

: The MIPS silicon always was a day late and a dollar short.

I disagree with "always" because the R2000 and R3000 products were pretty
good. The R6000 was a disaster and the R4000 was a year late and barely
made its clockspeed target.

: The R6000 fiasco was the icing on the cake. DEC planned a family of SMP
: boxes based on the R6000, and actually was three months from the planned
: 1990 announcement of a four-way R6000 system. DEC found out at the last
: moment that Control Data, who designed the MP hooks and real estate in
: the R6000 architecture, had an 18-month exclusive on the usage of the
: chip in SMP configurations. Oops... MIPS sorta kinda forgot to tell DEC
: about that piece of fine print!

I worked on the OS for the MIPS MP R6000. We worked closely with CDC on
software. I don't think CDC had much to do with the hardware design other
than customer input. They may have loaned some engineering to MIPS, but I
don't know. The machines they sold were manufactured by MIPS until the line
transfered to CDC after SGI bought MIPS.

I don't recall hearing anything about DEC working on MP R6000 machines.
Which isn't surprising since I expect they were planning to run Ultrix
anyway. Even if DEC were three months away from announcement, it probably
wouldn't have happened because BIT couldn't fab enough working chips for
even MIPS' rather small sales.

Its entirely possible that CDC bought an exclusive on the R6000 MP silicon
late in the game. MIPS was very good at coming up with an appropriate piece
of intellectual property to sell to an appropriate buyer when the end of
the quarter rolled around :-)

A more interesting question is how well DEC could have done designing their
own MIPS implementation. Say taking the team that did the 21064 and doing a
MIPS-III compatible part instead. (Ignoring the that MIPS was incredibly
closed mouth about the existence of MIPS-III and the fact that the R4000
implemented it.)

-Z-



