Henrik Klagges wrote:
> 
> Thomas Womack wrote:
> > Intel, on things like the Xeon, have had specfp and specint ratings
> > roughly equal.
> Yes, but on the x86 architecture! The whole x86 design is deliberately
> skewed on integer performance, because that's were the classic x86
> market is. It was an intelligent tradeoff for Intel to make: Given
> the x86 compatibility constraints, they couldn't be fast (compared to
> say Alpha) in Int and FP. So instead of being mediocre in both, they
> opted to be quite o.k. in Int (since the PPro).

   Don't forget that the x86 FPU model doesn't exactly lend itself
   to implementations that can run with the big boys.

> 
> > It's almost entirely conceivable that Merced might manage
> > to do the same -
> I don't think so. I think we'll see something like the int-to-fp
> relations of 1:2 or 1.3 we're accustomed to from RISC designs. My

   The SPECint95 to SPECfp95 ratio for RISCs ranges from about an x86
   like 0.7 or less for PowerPC to over 2.0 for POWER with Alpha, MIPS,
   SPARC, and PA-RISC clustering around 1.5 or so.  It looks like the
   next generation (EV6, PA8500, US-III) will be looking more like POWER
   in the 2.0 range.  Maybe this is from memory latency improving slower
   than bandwidth and/or branch related ILP limitation in CPUs with more
   integer execution units.
   
> guess for an 800 MHz Merced (not McK) are about 36 SpecInt and about
> 60 SpecFp.

   The Merced system bus will strongly dictate SPECfp95 and 60 probably
   isn't too far off.  I find it very interesting that Intel has been
   so secretive about the number and nature of IUs in Merced.  My guess
   is that it will have at 3 or 4 IUs plus two load/store units since
   aggressive use of if conversion means it will probably have to issue
   a lot of integer instructions that get squashed through predication.
   The SPECint95 scores will be very sensitive to compiler maturity but
   the academic research points out the possibilities of doing well on
   integer code with a high conditional branch content.  I wouldn't
   suprised to see int to fp ratio closer to 1.5 than 2.0 so that might
   put a Merced/800 SPECint95 score as high the low 40s.  The Merced's
   SPECint95 score broken down benchmark by benchmark will probably show
   huge variations.

