Jamie Fox (jamie@sageIT.com) wrote:

> I use a vt220 console on our Meridial phone system, and have an Epson LX-300
> serial/parallel dot-matrix printer I want to use on the terminal to print
> reports.  I've programmed the printer settings on the vt220 to
> 9600,7,none,1,Normal Mode,National Only,Full Page,No Terminator.  The printer
> settings are Interface=Serial, Buad=9600, Parity=None, Data=7, ETX/ACK=On,
> Software=ESC/P (as opposed to IBM 2380).  I'm using a serial printer cable from
> the 9-pin port on the VT220 marked "PR" to the 25-pin serial port on the
> printer.

OK, 2 suggestions:

1. ETX/ACK? You may want to look at using XON/XOFF flow control instead. 
   It's the only sort of flow control the printer port can deal with.

2. What sort of serial cable are you using between the printer and the
   terminal? I don't know what the pinout is for a 9-pin IBM PC serial 
   port, but it's probably different to the:

    Pin 1   Protective Ground 
    Pin 2   TX
    Pin 3   RX
    Pin 4   RTS
    Pin 5   DTR
    Pin 6   DSR
    Pin 7   GND

    of the VT220's printer port. You may also need to check to make sure 
    you don't need a null modem connector.

> I was told (by somebody who may or may not know what he's talking about) to 
use
> CTRL-F2 to toggle printing, but I get no print.

Use PRINT to get a single screen dump. Use Ctrl-PRINT to print each line 
as it arrives at your terminal. Use Printer Controller mode (via the setup 
menu) to channel everything from the host directly to the printer, with 
no intermediary interpreting or change of terminal display.

Finally, an added bonus - you now know you can trust whoever it was who 
told you about Ctrl-Print! >:)

Regards,

Simon.




