>         and shot down or confirmed.
> 
>         In May 1998, HP suddenly gets the Unix vision again:
> 
>                 "We heard from our customers, yada yada"
>                 "Merced is for NT we are extending PA to 8500 8700 and
>                 possibly 8900"

There are probably other reasons why HP suddenly returned to the UNIX 
fold and also extended the PA RISC roadmap.

1.	NT5's/NT2000's lateness means that without a major UNIX investment
	HP would have been without a credible Enterprise Server platform.

2.	They lost big chunks of Market Share in the Enterprise Server market 
	mainly to us (Sun) and it was beginning to hurt.

3.	IA64 was late.

4.	HP sees itself as an engineering company, they have always controlled 
	their own destiny, making systems, disks, CPUs etc. There were many 
	people in HP who felt that putting its architectural destiny in Intels 
	hands went against the culture of the company.

5.	They tried to sell PA->IA64 compatibility but no one believes this 
	and few analysts agree with HP's on this. Big HP Server Customers 
	needed a more sensible transition to Merced/Mkinley than the 
	sudden and "painless" cutover origionally envisioned.

>         Now what was that all about?  Well they are cranking away on McKinley,
>         their Merced research and simulations have been poured over.  Guess
>         what?  That dog won't hunt!!!  Plan B.
>

I would be cautious about suggesting that Mkinley wont work, most
analysts 
agree that Merced is late but that Mkinley which is being done by a 
different design team is on target. The real issue will be software 
availability. Merced being late reduces the software portfolio 
when Mkinley ships.


Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



