In article <36b93e58.0@news1.ibm.net>, 
"johnnywhizkid" <rodelro@ibm.net> writes:

> This problem started when the toner accidentally
> spilled over the unit.
> 
> Where can I locate the OPC Sensor ? Maybe I can start from there.

Yes, that sounds like the likely suspect.

   Remove the toner/OPC tray (and keep away from direct light).

   Remove the paper tray.

   About five inches in from the front of the printer, you
   will see an etched (frosted looking) metal roller about
   0.5" in diameter.   Just behind that, on the left side,
   you will see a black rectangular plastic object about
   1/4" x 3/8" x 4".   That is the OPC sensor (at least I
   think that is the right part, judging from the manual).

   Carefully clean it with a lint-free cloth, cotton swab,
   etc.   If there is a lot of toner or dust, try using
   a vacuum cleaner first.   CAUTION: Many vacuum cleaner
   bags will simply recycle fine toner right back into
   the air.

You should also *carefully* clean the gold mark on the edge
of the OPC belt.   Don't touch the surface of the belt,
and don't allow strong light to fall on the belt.

Once a year, I take the covers off my printer and vacuum
out all the dust, toner, and paper bits.   It helps to use
a soft brush to dislodge the dust while vacuuming.
Then I use a *slightly* damp cloth to wipe down all
accessable surfaces.

Since you had a toner spill, you might want to consider
this.   I suggest that you do it outdoors (after protecting
the OPC belt from light).

