Well, DEC and MIPS **did** partner for a while (DEC owned a small chunk
of MIPS, I believe) but DEC got burned one time too many.

The MIPS silicon always was a day late and a dollar short.

The R6000 fiasco was the icing on the cake. DEC planned a family of SMP
boxes based on the R6000, and actually was three months from the planned
1990 announcement of a four-way R6000 system. DEC found out at the last
moment that Control Data, who designed the MP hooks and real estate in
the R6000 architecture, had an 18-month exclusive on the usage of the
chip in SMP configurations. Oops... MIPS sorta kinda forgot to tell DEC
about that piece of fine print!




