Intel, on things like the Xeon, have had specfp and specint ratings
roughly equal. It's almost entirely conceivable that Merced might manage
to do the same - they've got space to hurl functional units at the
problem, after all, and some of specint is the sort of code with very
short intervals between branches that a really clever compiler might
have a field-day on if it can speculate across multiple branches - and
no-one, anywhere is talking about 50 specINT parts from anyone.

That's about the only way I can imagine to explain a 'Merced will toast
Alpha' statement. Just pick the right thing to compare on ...

