In article <78trqc$fpd9@hkpa05.polyu.edu.hk>, "Alexander K. M. Leung" <kmleung@fg702-6.abct.polyu.edu.hk> writes:
>
>
>    The data disk in our DEC Alpha 3000 workstation was damaged. I have
>purchased a disk to replace it. Can anyone give me the correct procedures to
>partition, format and mount the disk back to the system. The hard disk is a
>1.05GB RZ26L-VA hard disk. The OS in our workstation is DEC Unix 3.2c.

Digital UNIX supports two local file system and you have said which
you were using.  I haven't clue how you had the disk partitioned, so
I can't offer much detail on how to restore the partition table.  So,
allowing for what little information you're provided:

a.  Label the disk.  Being a data disk you don't need to worry about
    the boot block (which are different for the two file systems),
    but it doesn't hurt to get it right.  If you had UFS on the
    disk:

	# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz#c whatever

    For AdvFS:

	# disklabel -wr -t advfs /dev/rrz#c whatever

    In place of the "whatever" you can put the disk type.  Or, for
    well behaved and supported disks, use whatever you want and the
    disklabel command will get the geometry and default partition
    table from the driver.  For the '#' in the device, use the
    appropriate unit number:

	# = (bus * 8) + SCSI-id

2.  To repartition:

	# disklabel -e /dev/rrz#c

3.  Create the file system.  For UFS:

	# newfs /dev/rrz#x

     Here, I again use '#' for the device number number.  I also
     use an 'x' for the partition letter will be one of a though
     h.  If you used the whole disk it was probably 'c'.

     For AdvFS:

	# mkfdmn /dev/rz#c domain-name
	# mkfset domain-name fileset-name

4.  Mounting the file system, for UFS:

	# mount /dev/rz#x /mnt/wherever

    For AdvFS:

	# mount -t advfs domain-name#fileset-name /mnt/wherever


