Bill Broadley wrote:
[snip]
> : 2) Anyone have any memory throughput information on the XP1000. I'm
> : especially interested in the stream benchmark. It would be nice if
> : it were about as fast as the DS20 (about 1000 Mbytes/sec on the
> : stream benchmark).

  The DS20 CPU boards use 8 tsunami "D" chips to effectively
  implement a 2 x 256 bit (plus ECC) wide memory.  The XP1000 is
  a bit more economical with only 4 (these things are 304 BGAs)
  "D" chips for a 256 bit (plus ECC) wide memory.

  I haven't seen stream numbers for the XP1000 but the difference
  in SPECfp95 scores (58.7 for the DS20 vs either 49 or 52 - both
  have been touted for the XP1000) suggests the effective CPU
  memory bandwidth is somewhat reduced by the narrower memory.

  The real suprise to me was that the DS20's LINPACK 1000x1000
  MFLOPs number wasn't much higher than a 21164/533 server.  I
  guess there's a lot of work left to be done tuning LINPACK
  for the EV6.

  BTW, both the DS20 and XP1000 use the lowest grade EV6 L2 cache
  technology, 166 MHz SRAMs (I guess there will be a glut of these
  now that the Pentium II/333 is falling out of favour).  The API
  EV6 board advertised on their web site uses 200 MHz late write
  SDRAMs IIRC (the next step up).  The EV6 should look even better
  when Motorola, Cypress etc can ramp up the double data rate or
  DDR SDRAMs which can support the full 333 MHz x 128 bit capability
  of the L2 port.

> 
> I'd be very interested in similiar, as well as dual cpu numbers
> for the XP1000.

  The XP1000 supports only one CPU.  The dual CPU workstation
  (the anaconda?) apparently has an uncertain future and may not
  be released on schedule.

[snip]
> Bill

All opinions strictly my own.
