When you say the monitor cable is "3-pin", do you mean the video connector
has 3 very small coaxial-style connectors inside a D-shell ?  If so, this
is the connector used by DEC video cards which operate at higher refresh
rates (66/72Hz) than the VR299 can take (it's 66Hz).  The older video cards
which are suitable for this monitor (and the only one which was an option in
a 4000/60 also had an SVGA output) used 15-pin D-connectors.

I don't recognise the numbers on the graphics adapter, but at a guess you
need a monitor capable of 1280x1024 @66Hz.  (Or better.)

If someone sold you all this as a working whole, they misled you.

