I have just read the latest Microprocessor Report after looking
forward to the annual RISCie awards and general year roundup for
a while.  Where does MDR's prejudice against Alpha come from?
Is this the ghost of Peter Christie lingering on or what?

On the graph showing current and projected SPECfp95_base they
apparently projected higher clock rate EV6 using the 8400 up-
grade board as the base.  They project the EV6/600 at 51 or 52
when the DS20 shows with the proper chip set the EV6/500 gets
48.4 Granted, they probably didn't have the DS20 numbers at
the time but perhaps they could have looked at the L2 cache
and memory architecture of the 8400 upgrade vs what a true EV6
system would consist of.  The really insulting thing is they
project the EV6/750 tied with the UltraSPARC-III/600 at 60 SPECfp95_base! When
has Sun *EVER* shipped a new microarchit-
ecture that met either its clock target or performance target?

Finally they gave the PA8500 the award as best RISC processor
citing its "high performance" (hadn't seen the C360 scores
yet obviously), "use of L2 on-die cache" (apparently coming
in at a monstrous 475 mm2 die size to do this didn't have
much impact).  They also praised it for meeting its performance
target (it actually slipped per clock performance significantly)
and shipping within a month of its target.  Yesireee, that
innovative 8500 was a real challenge - shrinking an 8200 core
and adding SRAM.  Not like that cut and paste 21264, heck what
was so great about that? a completely new microarchitecture,
most complex OOO design yet highest clock rate despite 0.35um
process.  And innovations like adaptive strategy branch pred-
iction, integer unit clustering, adaptive load speculation with
partial pipeline flush recovery, and entirely new system bus
interface.  Maybe Compaq can ship the 21264 in five different
pastel coloured packages and win the award next year  :-<

BTW, the latest gospel according to Intelprocessor Support
is that the Merced will now met or exceed the EV67/1000 in
performance in late 2000.  I guess MDR is now projecting all
Alpha scores based on the 8400 6/575.  Maybe they should go
back and carefully read their own back issues (Ever heard
of tsumani or DDR SRAM?).

All opinions, gripes, grumbles, and beefs strictly my own.
--
Paul W. DeMone       The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race of EPIC
Kanata, Ontario      proportions to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com    architectures with MIPSed results but ALPHA's well
pdemone@igs.net      that ends well.



