John Savard wrote:

> Isn't the Alpha an Intel chip now? I thought that technology got sold
> to Intel as part of a settlement on a patent case, just before Compaq
> bought out DEC.

Oh, no, not again...

No, Intel did not "buy" Alpha. Intel got three things out of the
settlement:
cross-licensing of CPU-related patents, all of DIGITAL's fabrication
facilities, and a license to manufacture Alphas.

DIGITAL - now Compaq - still owns the Alpha architecture. It got some cash
in exchange for the fabs, patent cross-licensing, and an obligation from
Intel
to fabricate Alphas for them, if desired, for a certain number of years.

