Yep. The 8400 has been around for so long now that looking
beyond it should have been almost a no-brainer for MDR.

> system would consist of.  The really insulting thing is they
> project the EV6/750 tied with the UltraSPARC-III/600 at 60 SPECfp95_base! 
That's a good one! Haha! US3 delivering more fp/MHz than EV6! US3
would deserve an applause if it would get *even* with EV6 on fp/MHz
(or any other measure).

> Finally they gave the PA8500 the award as best RISC processor
> citing its "high performance" (hadn't seen the C360 scores
> yet obviously), 
IMHO, the C360 integer scores are o.k. FP is a blip due to the memory
subsystem.

> "use of L2 on-die cache" 
That should be more of a criterion to give an award to a process 
technology designer, not a CPU designer. Anyone with an advanced 
process can put a big L2 cache on a die and reap the benefits.

> They also praised it for meeting its performance
> target (it actually slipped per clock performance significantly)
> and shipping within a month of its target.  
I still think that praise is deserved, especially for the shipping 
date. The slips in the Xeon (6 months depending how you count) and 
the 21264 (6-9 months depending how you count) have hurt some. The 
IA64 slip has hurt a lot and continues to do so.

> that innovative 8500 was a real challenge - shrinking an 8200 core
> and adding SRAM.  
I think it was neat that HP needed nothing more to reach the level
of performance the 8500 delivers. It also suggests that the 8200
core was good to begin with (it got good performance from it's
0.5 micron process).

> [Alpha]
> most complex OOO design yet highest clock rate despite 0.35um
> process.  
Agreed. Plus, retrofitting the 0OO design onto an instruction set &
architecture that always was optimized as a speed demon. Plus, getting
the OOO design right the first time, i.e. not only making it work,
but earning a huge performance multiplier (about 2) as well.

For comparison, it's not clear yet whether Intel will make EPIC
work the first time, nor whether the performance multiplier from it
will be bigger than 1.

[I'm getting ready for a renewed round of CPU wars :-), see below]

So, my personal list of current RISC awards (for design beauty, number 
of new ideas + performance) looks more like this (1/99):

1.   Alpha 21264
