In article <36b8bdc1.16892893@news.prosurfr.com>, jsavard@tenMAPSONeerf.edmonton.ab.ca (John Savard) writes:

> Isn't the Alpha an Intel chip now? I thought that technology got sold
> to Intel as part of a settlement on a patent case, just before Compaq
> bought out DEC.

No, Intel was forced by the agreement to FAB Alpha chips _for_DEC_,
relieving DEC of their need to finance a FAB plant.  Intel does
not get the Alpha designs, although they do get cross-licensing
rights if any _Intel_ chips happen to use patents obtained for
the Alpha (which is how the whole lawsuit got started).

DEC/Compaq can also buy Alpha chips from other FAB companies, and
likely will do so if Intel vaporchip speeds don't meet Samsung
vaporchip speeds.

